regress/artifacts\lcashiftub\pass2_c7d7414\codex.txt:1310:28227:   "state_note": "*** RECORDS CORRECTION 2026-09-06, from RE-DERIVING this row's own citations instead of trusting them.  THE\nHEADLINE COUNT (eleven live terminating calls) IS RIGHT.  THE ENUMERATION UNDER IT IS WRONG IN TWO WAYS, AND THE\nFIRST ONE CHANGES THE FIX DESIGN. ***\n\n**1.  `dev >= 21` IS NOT A LIVE KILL.  IT IS ALREADY DE-FATALISED -- AND IT IS THIS FILE'S OWN FIX PRECEDENT.**\n\nThe row lists it among \"two range guards that also kill\".  The source says otherwise: the guard is at `:127`, its\nbody is a COMMENTED-OUT `fatal()` + `exit(1)` at `:128-129`, and what actually executes is `dev = 0;` at `:130`.\n\n```c\n\tif (dev >= 21) {\n\t\t/*  fatal(\"[ LCA: No bus 0 device? TODO ]\\n\");\n\t\texit(1);  */\n\t\tdev = 0;\n\t}\n```\n\nSomeone has already made, in this very file, the decision this row exists to make: **delete the kill, substitute a\nsafe default, keep going.**  That is a TRANSFERRED WARRANT from inside the same file, which the six-member\noption-set rule says to go looking for -- *\"grep the fork for an existing idiom; the tree's own precedents are part\nof the design space\"* -- and which nobody had found because the row was read rather than re-derived.  So only ONE\nrange guard is live: `ofs > len` at `:163`.\n\n**2.  THE ENUMERATION COVERED 8 OF THE 11, AND THE THREE IT MISSED ARE NOT VALUE GUARDS AT ALL.**\n\nRe-derived by `grep -n 'exit(1)' dev_lca.c` minus the commented one (12 matches, 11 live), the eleven are:\n\n| line | class | condition |\n|---|---|---|\n| 73  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_assert` -- bare `fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1)`, **NO GUARD** |\n| 85  | **INTERRUPT STUB** | `lca_interrupt_deassert` -- likewise |\n| 134 | BUS | `bus != 0`, i.e. `d->ioc_conf & 1` |\n| 163 | RANGE | `ofs > len` |\n| 206 | VALUE | `idata & ~1` (IOC_CONF) |\n| 226 | VALUE | `idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN` |\n| 241 | VALUE | `idata != 0 && idata != 0x300800000` |\n| 256 | VALUE | `idata != 0x700000` |\n| 280 | VALUE | `idata != 0x240000000` |\n| 295 | VALUE | `idata != 0x3ff00000` |\n| 315 | CATCH-ALL | `default:` -- any unimplemented ioc offset, read OR write |\n\n**THE TWO INTERRUPT STUBS ARE PLAUSIBLY THE MOST SEVERE OF THE ELEVEN, AND A ROW FRAMED AS A VALUE-GUARD CLASS\nCOULD NOT SEE THEM.**  They are not a wrong value being written -- they are an ENTIRE UNIMPLEMENTED PATH with no\npredicate at all, so *any* device asserting an interrupt through the LCA ends the process regardless of what it\nwrites.  The four \"accepts exactly one value\" registers need a firmware revision that writes something else; these\ntwo need only that an interrupt is asserted.\n\n*A row named after one class hides the members of the others.  That is this project's cell-not-class pattern\nwearing an enumeration instead of a detector -- the same shape the m88kexcdeliver chain hit four times.*\n\n**WHAT IS UNCHANGED:** the severity (HIGH), the single consumer (`machine_alpha.c`, `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`),\nthe absence of any alpha rig, and the observation that a guest here is FIRMWARE so the reaching value is whatever\nSRM or NetBSD writes.  The scope caveat about range-vs-value guards also stands and is now sharper, because there\nis only one live range guard to reason about.\n\n--- the original note follows, unaltered ---\n\n\n=== DESIGN PASS, 2026-09-06 (codex / GPT-6 Astra at max, read-only).  Answer preserved at\n=== regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_design_20260906.txt.  IT CORRECTED THREE OF THE FIVE PREMISES I HAD\n=== JUST RE-DERIVED, and one of the corrections changes what this round may even choose from.\n\n**SEVERITY ORDER CONFIRMED: the two INTERRUPT STUBS first** -- but \"provisionally; firmware execution order is\nunproved\", which is the honest qualifier my own framing lacked.\n\n*** A SEVENTH OPTION-SET MEMBER: IMPLEMENT. ***  The carrier's set is DELETE / DECLINE / DECLINE+LATCHED\nANNOUNCE / ACCEPT / FAULT / BACKED ACCEPT -- all six presume the access is a REFUSAL DECISION.  For the two\ninterrupt stubs none of them applies: *\"declining an interrupt leaves the machine incomplete.\"*  A stub is not a\nrefusal to service a value; it is an unimplemented PATH, and the disposition is to write the code.  This is the\noption-set completeness rule paying off exactly as it is meant to -- the member was found by a seat reading the\ntree, not by argument.\n\n*** AND THE FAULT MEMBER IS NOT AVAILABLE ON THIS MACHINE AT ALL. ***  `dev_unreadable.c` works because\n`memory_rw.c` dispatches an exception on a failed device access **for MIPS and M88K, with NO ALPHA EQUIVALENT**.\nSo the idiom this fork installs ten times in `machine_sgi.c` does not transfer here, and any round reaching for\nFAULT owes Alpha exception delivery first.  *A precedent that is real elsewhere in the tree is not thereby\ntransferable; that is the warrant-transfer rule, and it took a seat opening `memory_rw.c` to see it.*\n\n**AND A FINDING ABOUT THE ALPHA PORT THAT OUTRANKS THIS ROW:** the Alpha interrupt callbacks merely set\n`irq_asserted`, and **the execution loop never consumes that flag**.  So implementing the two stubs would not by\nitself deliver an interrupt -- removing the exits cannot complete the path.  Filed as its own question rather\nthan folded in here.\n\n## THE PREMISE AUDIT, and I was wrong three times\n\n* **PREMISE 1 HOLDS.**  Twelve textual occurrences, eleven outside comments.\n* **PREMISE 2's REMOVAL IS REAL; ITS \"USABLE PRECEDENT\" CONCLUSION IS WRONG.**  I read the commented-out\n  `dev >= 21` kill plus its live `dev = 0` as the file's own answer, transferable to this round.  It is not: it\n  **ALIASES \"no selected device\" to DEVICE ZERO**, which is harmless today only because device 7 is the sole\n  installed device and slot 0 happens to be empty.  Populate slot 0 and it is a latent alias defect.  *So the\n  question I asked -- is this a sound precedent or a latent defect of its own -- has the second answer, and my\n  brief had already leaned on the first.*\n* **PREMISE 3's CLASS COUNTS HOLD, ITS SINGLETON CLAIM DOES NOT.**  `W_BASE0` accepts **TWO** values, `0` and\n  `0x300800000` (`idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL`), so \"four registers accepting exactly ONE value\" is\n  wrong -- only **three** window guards accept a single value.  *This one is mine twice over: the original row\n  note said it, I re-derived the enumeration tonight and reproduced it, and the guard's own text was in my\n  terminal when I did.*\n* **PREMISE 4 HOLDS** as one machine-setup consumer (AlphaBook 1), with no dedicated boot rig -- **but\n  \"never exercised\" DOES NOT FOLLOW**: a generic constructor sweep exists (`gate_asan_sweep.sh`), without\n  proving coverage of these branches.  My \"no rig means no coverage\" was too strong.\n* **PREMISE 5 IS AN UNSUPPORTED RESTRICTION.**  I said a guest here is firmware, so the reaching value is\n  whatever SRM or NetBSD writes.  Guest KERNEL stores use the same MMIO path, and interrupt callbacks arise from\n  DEVICE events -- neither is firmware.\n\n## DISPOSITIONS IT WOULD TAKE, per class\n* **INTERRUPT STUB (2):** IMPLEMENT forwarding and CPU interrupt delivery.\n* **BUS (1):** implement Type-1 decoding then ACCEPT through `bus_pci`, using its existing absent-device\n  handling.  `ioc_conf & 1` selects the CONFIGURATION-CYCLE TYPE and is **not the decoded bus number** -- and\n  deleting this exit alone leaves `dev` uninitialised.\n* **RANGE (1):** DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE with initialised read results and no forwarded side effects.  Note\n  `ofs` is at most three, **so this guard cannot fire for four- or eight-byte accesses** -- changing `>`\n  mechanically is not a design.\n* **VALUE (6):** ACCEPT documented register fields using the existing backing, latched announce for the missing\n  DMA/TLB effects.  **Every guarded assignment already happens BEFORE its check**, so no invented backing is\n  needed -- but readback alone does not implement translation.\n* **CATCH-ALL (1):** split documented registers (`STAT0`/`STAT1`) from genuine holes; FAULT only for confirmed\n  holes and only once Alpha exception delivery exists.\n\n**WHAT IT COULD NOT ESTABLISH, said plainly:** source shows connected interrupt paths and conditional MMIO paths\nfor the other nine sites, but **no particular SRM/NetBSD execution of any terminating site**.  Firmware\nreachability and failure frequency remain unmeasured, so the severity order is provisional on exactly that.\n\n\n*** THE SEVERITY WARRANT THIS ROW WAS MISSING: THE REJECTED VALUES INCLUDE A DOCUMENTED ONE, AND THE\nSTORE LANDS BEFORE THE PROCESS ENDS.  Found by the codex seat 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt),\nverified against the source and the header before recording. ***\n\nEach of the three window guards has this shape:\n\n    d->window_mask_0 = idata;                          <- the store LANDS\n    if (idata != 0x700000ULL) { fatal(...); exit(1); } <- then the process ends\n\n**And zero is a documented encoding.**  From src/include/thirdparty/alpha_lcareg.h:\n\n    #define IOC_W_MASK_1M   0x0000000000000000ULL   /* 1MB window */\n\nSo a guest selecting the SMALLEST DOCUMENTED WINDOW SIZE ends the emulator.  The same argument covers\nW_BASE1, whose one accepted constant 0x240000000 carries IOC_W_BASE_WEN (0x200000000, *Window enable*) --\n**so a driver DISABLING a window, by writing a base with WEN clear, is killed too.**\n\n*** THIS IS #504's WARRANT AT A SECOND DEVICE: documented values that the model kills on. ***  It is not a\njunk-value guard; it is a whitelist of ONE that excludes documented members of its own encoding.  codex adds\nthat all three registers start at zero through constructor initialisation, so the rejected value is also the\nRESET value.\n\n**AND THE RANGE SITE IS FAR MORE REACHABLE THAN THIS ROW SAID.**  It was recorded as `ofs > len'.  Reading\nDEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa): `relative_addr >>= 5; ofs = relative_addr & 3;' -- **ofs is guest ADDRESS BITS 5-6**,\nnot a written value.  So an ordinary 1-byte access at an address with those bits set to 2 or 3 ends the\nprocess.  That is address decoding, not a value whitelist, and it belongs in a different disposition class\nfrom the six value guards.  The rest of that function is filed separately as [[lcaisalane]].\n\n*** DISPOSITION PASS, codex 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_disposition_20260907.txt).  IT NARROWS MY REACHABILITY CLAIM,\nCONFIRMS TWO OTHERS, AND SUPPLIES THE IN-TREE PRECEDENTS THE OPTION-SET RULE ASKS FOR -- INCLUDING\nCONFLICTING ONES, WHICH IS WHAT I ASKED IT TO SURFACE. ***\n\nVerdict: *\"Disposition 5 is unavailable through Alpha's failed-device-access path.  Five candidate rounds fit\na uniform VALUE-refusal policy.  The tree supplies CONFLICTING precedents, especially for lane handling and\nstoring unsupported values.\"*\n\n*** MY `AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE ENDS THE EMULATOR' CLAIM IS TOO BROAD, AND I HAVE REPEATED IT IN THREE\nPLACES. ***  codex: *\"they read zero INITIALLY, then echo accepted writes.  Writing back the initial zero\nkills the last three; an arbitrary RMW does not necessarily kill them -- its resulting value matters.\"*\n\n**The precise claim is the INITIAL-ZERO readback**: before any accepted write, `W_MASK0', `W_BASE1' and\n`W_MASK1' read 0 and accept only one non-zero constant each, so a read-modify-write performed on a\nfreshly-reset device writes 0 back and ends the process.  **Once a valid value has been written the register\nechoes it**, and an RMW of that value is accepted.  So the window is early -- driver probe or\ninitialisation -- rather than any RMW at any time.  That is still a routine thing for a driver to do at\nattach, and it is still reached with a documented encoding (`IOC_W_MASK_1M` = 0), but the sentence I wrote\nwas wider than the fact.\n\n**CONFIRMED, both of which I had asserted:** every VALUE arm STORES BEFORE CHECKING; and the accepted sets\nare exactly as filed (`IOC_CONF' {0,1}, `IOC_TB_ENA' {0,0x80}, `W_BASE0' {0,0x300800000}, and the three\nsingle-value ones).\n\n**DISPOSITION 5 IS UNAVAILABLE -- now established from a third direction.**  The carrier already recorded it\ngenerally, [[alphafaultclaim]] reached it from the instruction side, and this reaches it from the\nfailed-device-access path.  **Any proposal for a bus-error refusal on an Alpha device owes Alpha exception\ndelivery first**, which is a far larger round than the one it would be riding on.\n\n**THE PRECEDENT I ASKED FOR, AND IT IS AN EXACT ONE:** `cpu_sh.c' defines `sh3_cpu_interrupt_assert()' and\n`sh3_cpu_interrupt_deassert()' with EMPTY `/* TODO */' bodies -- *\"an exact unimplemented-callback precedent\nfor 2, ignore silently\"* -- and `core/interrupt.c' defines empty `no_interrupt_assert/deassert' callbacks,\nthough those represent intentionally absent connections rather than unimplemented ones.  **So the two\nINTERRUPT STUBS have a direct in-tree answer** and do not need disposition 7 after all, which is what I had\nassumed they needed.\n\n**AND IT REPORTS THE PRECEDENTS CONFLICT** -- especially on lane handling and on whether to store an\nunsupported value.  That is the more useful answer: it means the choice is genuinely open at those sites\nrather than settled by the tree, so a round there must argue rather than transfer.\n\n**SCOPE: FIVE candidate rounds** under a uniform VALUE-refusal policy -- consistent with the earlier read's\n*\"this needs several rounds\"* and finer than it.\n\n**WHERE THE OVER-BROAD FORM SURVIVES, named because it cannot be edited:** commit `de96f11`'s MESSAGE\nsays *\"each reads back 0 while its guard accepts one non-zero constant, and AN ORDINARY READ-MODIFY-WRITE\nENDS THE EMULATOR on all three\"*.  A commit message is immutable, so the house rule -- correct the false\nrecord IN PLACE -- cannot be followed literally.  **The next best thing is to point at it from the record a\nreader will reach**, which is this row.  The narrowed claim above is the operative one: the kill needs the\nINITIAL zero readback, not any RMW at any time.\n\n*** THE PREREQUISITE IS DISCHARGED.  `#506` LANDED THIS AFTERNOON. ***\n\nThis row's HOLD-Q asked: *\"given that every row must match the `fatal()` TEXT rather than the exit\nstatus, and given that `lcaconfwidth` means the IOC_CONF arm's text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the\ntext-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must `lcaconfwidth`'s printf fix land FIRST, making it a\nprerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?\"*\n\n**It landed.**  `#506` repaired both narrowing sites in `dev_lca.c` -- `:205` (IOC_CONF) and the\n`default:` arm -- to `0x%\" PRIx64` with `(uint64_t)idata`, matching the five siblings.  Measured by\n`regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py`: **HEAD 4/6 -> fix 6/6, same script**, with `kill width` now reading\n`0x100000000` instead of `0x0`.\n\n> **So the text oracle is now sound at the IOC_CONF arm, and the ordering question in the HOLD-Q is\n> answered by events rather than by adjudication.**  What remains of that HOLD-Q is its SECOND half,\n> untouched: *the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger's earlier\n> census dropped `W_BASE1` and `W_MASK1` -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four.*\n\n*The interrupt-stub confound is also unchanged and still governs the witness design:\n`lca_interrupt_assert` and `lca_interrupt_deassert` are bare `fatal()`+`exit(1)` with the ISA PIC\nchain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the same status as the value guards under\ntest.  That is why the rows must match text, which is why `#506` was the prerequisite.*\n\n*** THE HOLD-Q'S SECOND HALF IS ANSWERED BY MEASUREMENT: THE ACCEPT-ROW SET IS SIX. ***\nEnumerated by reading `if (idata` in `dev_lca.c`, 2026-09-07:\n\n    :203  IOC_CONF   idata & ~1\n    :231  TB_ENA     idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN\n    :246  W_BASE0    idata != 0ULL && idata != 0x300800000ULL\n    :261  W_MASK0    idata != 0x700000ULL\n    :285  W_BASE1    idata != 0x240000000ULL\n    :300  W_MASK1    idata != 0x3ff00000ULL\n\n**Six, not four** -- the earlier census in this row dropped `W_BASE1` and `W_MASK1`, exactly as the\nHOLD-Q suspected.  *Note two shapes, which may matter for the fix design: two BITMASK tests\n(`& ~mask`) and four EQUALITY tests, one of which accepts two values.*\n\n**And the eleven-live count is confirmed rather than carried:** `grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);'`\ngives **12**, of which `:129` sits inside a commented-out block -- so 11 live, in **FIVE** classes:\n2 interrupt stubs, 1 bus/cycle-type, 1 range/lane, 6 value, 1 default.\n\n> #### *** THIS SENTENCE SAID \"four classes\" AND THEN LISTED FIVE.  Corrected 2026-09-07 after the\n> #### grok design pass named it -- and the same slip was in the brief that asked it to. ***\n> The opener asks seats to check enumerations; mine was wrong in its own headline, with the\n> refutation printed three words later.  *A count and the list beside it are two claims, and\n> nothing checks them against each other.*\n\n*** AND THE FIRST HALF OF THE HOLD-Q IS DISCHARGED BY EVENTS: `#506` LANDED (`7a41b3f`). ***\nThe question was whether `lcaconfwidth`'s printf fix had to land first, since this row's rows must\nmatch the `fatal()` TEXT rather than the exit status (the two interrupt stubs kill with the same\nstatus as every value guard).  **It landed and is measured** -- both narrowing sites now print\n`0x%\" PRIx64` with `(uint64_t)idata`, HEAD 4/6 -> fix 6/6 -- so the IOC_CONF arm's text is sound.\n\n**Design brief out to grok** (`regress/artifacts/lcabadval/DESIGN_brief.md`) rather than codex: the\ncodex seat refused the sibling `lcaconfwidth` census on its content filter, and a brief enumerating\neleven host kills is the same recorded trigger shape.\n\n*** GROK DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07: **NOT RATIFY** as a six-armed one-design round until two of my\nclaims are dropped.  The ROUND SHAPE survives; two warrants and one count do not. ***\n\n**1. \"THE ACCEPT-ROW SET IS SIX\" STILL UNDER-COUNTS.**  De-escalation owes a row per PREDICATE, but\nthree of the six predicates accept TWO values:\n\n    IOC_CONF  {0, 1}          TB_ENA   {0, 0x80}      W_BASE0  {0, 0x300800000}\n    W_MASK0   {0x700000}      W_BASE1  {0x240000000}  W_MASK1  {0x3ff00000}\n\n**Nine pinning values, not six rows** -- and three of the nine are the reset `0`, which is vacuous\nunless sequenced after a nonzero write.  *So replacing the old \"four\" with \"six\" corrected one\nunder-count into another.*\n\n**2. S5 DOES NOT TRANSFER, AND CITING IT WOULD CITE A DEFECT AS A WARRANT.**  I offered the live\n`dev = 0` path (`dev >= 21`, kill commented out) as this file's own *delete-the-kill, substitute-a-\nsafe-default* precedent.  **It is PCI type-0 TAG DECODE, not a register-value policy**: it aliases\n\"no device selected\" onto SLOT 0, which is empty today only because the installed SIO is device 7.\n**And that loop is already filed as [[lcashiftub]] -- `0x800 << 20` in a signed `int` at `dev == 20`\nis UB.**  Substituting `0x700000` when firmware wrote `IOC_W_MASK_1M` (0) would be that same alias\npointed at a documented encoding.\n\n> **THE RIGHT IN-FILE WARRANT IS `W_T_BASE0` / `W_T_BASE1`**: the sibling TRANSLATED-window registers\n> already store any value and do not kill.  *Base/mask killing on a firmware snapshot while\n> translated-base accepts everything is the file's own outlier* -- which is a far stronger warrant\n> than the one I proposed, and one grep away.\n\n**3. THE ROUND SHAPE IS RATIFIED: ONE ROUND, MEMBER 4 ACCEPT, ALL SIX.**  Delete `fatal`+`exit` and\nkeep the store that already ran.  Five of the six fields are pure storage (`window_base_*`,\n`window_mask_*`, `tlb_enable` appear only as `odata = d->X` / `d->X = idata`); the sixth, `ioc_conf`,\nis consumed only as `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` in `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`, **so extra CONF bits\nare already discarded at the only consumer** and a masked store would be an unlicensed addition.\n**Bitmask vs equality is a DETECTOR-SHAPE split, not a fix-design split.**  FAULT is unavailable on\nAlpha.\n\n*** 4. THE FLOOR-(a) TRAP, and it is the finding that most changes the detector: AN ACCEPT-ONLY\nDETECTOR IS GREEN ON HEAD. ***  Writing the six magic constants SURVIVES on the committed build.  So\na detector that writes only currently-legal values **is green under verbatim reinstatement of all six\noriginal guards** -- the vacuity-floor miss in its purest form.  **The rows that make (b) bite are the\ndocumented-REJECTED writes**: MASK0 `0`, BASE1 with WEN clear, CONF bit 32, a non-TEN TB_ENA bit.\n\n**5. THE (d) FORMS HERE ARE SITE OMISSION AND DISJUNCT-DROPPING, not an operand swap.**  Leave\n`W_MASK1` (or `W_BASE1`) fatal while de-fatalising the other five -- incomplete clause (b), and\n**`ioc rt` in `lca_confwidth_probe.py` stays green because nothing in that probe touches MASK1.**\nSecond form: on `W_BASE0`, drop `idata != 0ULL &&` and keep the magic comparison -- the magic accept\nrow stays green, *which is exactly why BASE0 owes TWO pins*.\n\n**6. `lca_confwidth_probe.py`'s `kill fired` and `died` WANTS WILL INVERT IF THIS ROUND SHIPS.**  It\nis a `#506` witness, not this round's detector.  **Re-pin it under the oracle-correction rule -- do\nnot launder by editing wants after the product change.**\n\n**7. THE STUBS ARE A SIBLING, NOT A PREREQUISITE.**  Construction does not fire them today (PIC\n`ier`/`irr` start 0, 8253 `in_use=0`, WDC only with an IDE image, ns16550 needs `com_ier` bits), and\n`#506`'s legal session already survived on this machine.  **Text is sufficient to ATTRIBUTE a kill\nand is NOT a survival oracle** -- an accept row wanting `alive` still false-reds if a stub fires, and\nthat is controlled by SESSION SHAPE (cold prompt, `step`, don't touch the ISA window), not by\nde-fatalising the stubs first.  *A BOOT witness would make them a prerequisite, because `timer_stop`\nis why stepping does not tick the PIT.*\n\n**8. AND AN HONESTY CONSTRAINT FOR THE CONTRACT:** `default:` still kills `STAT0`/`STAT1`, which the\nheader documents as R/W1C.  **This round can be green and firmware can still die**, so the contract\nmust name that as out of scope or the round will be reported as a false pass of *\"LCA no longer\nhost-kills on IOC MMIO\"*.\n\n**Split out, each its own round:** the interrupt stubs (member 7); `bus != 0` -- *deleting that\n`exit(1)` leaves `dev` UNINITIALISED in the `else`*, which is the sketch rule and not a rider;\n`ofs > len` (already [[lcaisalane]]; `ofs` is address bits 5-6); and the `default:` arm.  **HAE at\n`case LCA_IOC_BASE` already silently ignores writes to a documented register -- that is member 2, not\n4, and must not be unified with the six.**\n\n*** 2026-09-07, CODEX FIX-CHECK: QUALIFIED YES, AND IT FOUND A COUNTEREXAMPLE TO THE ROUND'S CENTRAL WARRANT. ***  All six stores confirmed prior to their guards (table, full denominator); the consumer analysis confirmed with one sharpening -- masking CONF would change READBACK policy, not merely bus selection.  **But `dev_sh4.c's RCR1 comment records that its store USED TO run before the exit and was DELIBERATELY MOVED BELOW THE GUARD**, so \"the store already ran\" is not a general warrant in this tree.  The round now carries an open A-vs-B design question (keep the store vs decline the write) that the W_T_BASE* siblings CANNOT settle, since accepting-anything there may be an absent guard rather than a decision.  Disposition set also grew by two source-backed members (accept/decline WITH REPEATED announcements; complete with a GUEST-VISIBLE DEVICE ERROR, dev_wdc.c's WDCE_ABRT+interrupt).  And a correction to my own brief: only TWO BITS IN STAT0 are R/W1C, not STAT1.\n\n*** TITLE CORRECTED 2026-09-07: it said FOUR registers and the true count is SIX. *** Derived, not recalled: `exit(1)` occurs SEVEN times in DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) -- six guarded value-arms (LCA_IOC_CONF :215, TB_ENA :235, W_BASE0 :250, W_MASK0 :265, W_BASE1 :289, W_MASK1 :304) and the `default:` arm at :324, which this round does NOT close.  The four window registers are presumably what the row was opened on; CONF and TB_ENA carry the same shape and were folded in later without the title following.  **A stale title is how a queue row is READ** -- the brief that goes to the panel says six, and a reader comparing the two would have had to guess which was right.  W_T_BASE0 (:272) and W_T_BASE1 (:311) confirmed UNGUARDED: plain readback/store, no value test -- which is the sibling warrant the design brief puts to the panel.\n\n*** AND THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE WAS ITSELF CORRUPTED WHEN FIRST WRITTEN -- BY THE SHELL, QUIETLY\nENOUGH TO SURVIVE REVIEW. ***  It was applied through `python -c \"...\"` with DOUBLE quotes, and\nbash performed COMMAND SUBSTITUTION on the backticks inside it: `exit(1)` and `default:` were\nEXECUTED and replaced by their empty output, leaving readable prose with two words silently\nmissing -- \"Derived, not recalled:  occurs SEVEN times\".  bash did print two errors, but python\nran on the mangled source anyway and the write SUCCEEDED, so the record was wrong while the\ncommand reported success.\n\n**Inside a double-quoted shell argument a backtick is a COMMAND, not a quote mark.**  That is the\ncarrier's backslash-through-heredoc class arriving on a new character, and with the same\nsignature: the failure lands in the FILE, not in the exit status.\n\n*** AND THE REPAIR FAILED TWICE MORE BEFORE IT LANDED, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. ***  The\nfix was retried as `python -c '...'` with SINGLE quotes -- correct for backticks -- and bash\nrejected it, because the note explaining the hazard contains the word \"carrier's\", whose\nAPOSTROPHE terminated the shell quote.  So: double quotes lose to backticks, single quotes lose to\napostrophes, and English prose about shell quoting reliably contains both.\n\n**The rule is not a better quoting style.  It is: PROSE DESTINED FOR A RECORD GOES INTO A SCRIPT\nFILE, never through `-c`.**  This paragraph was written that way and landed intact on the first\nattempt.\n\n*** PASS-1 DESIGN PANEL 2026-09-07 (preserved: regress/artifacts/lcabadval/panel_p1_20260907/ -- _scratchpad is never committed).  PROVISIONAL: FIVE OF THE\nLANDED SEATS ARE READ HERE AND **CODEX WAS STILL STREAMING** WHEN THIS WAS WRITTEN (its file grew\n14,943 -> 30,594 bytes between two reads and its tail is mid-`exec`, not a verdict).  Any ruling\nshort of the roster is provisional on its face, so no disposition is adopted below. ***\n\nSEATS: grok, glm, deepseek, minimax and the kimi-k3:cloud SUBSTITUTE answered (5); codex IN FLIGHT;\nthe kimi CLI WALLED on an explicit 403 weekly limit, so that seat is COVERED, not lost; agy\nDELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (measurement reserve, owner ruling 2026-08-30); opus5 and fable5 NOT FIRED,\nreserved for the 2026-09-09 adjudication.\n\n*** THE PANEL'S DOMINANT FINDING IS THAT MY BRIEF POSED THE WRONG QUESTION, AND FOUR SEATS REACHED\nIT INDEPENDENTLY: THE FORK IS MIS-POSED AS ONE DISPOSITION FOR ALL SIX ARMS. ***\n\nThe kimi substitute put it sharpest, and it is a self-inflicted wound: *\"The fork as posed (one\nanswer for the file) repeats the exact error the #447 census inlines a warning against.\"*  **My\nbrief QUOTED `#447`'s own sentence -- \"Saying 'the store is upstream' once for the file would have\nbeen wrong three times out of four.  Check it per site.\" -- and then asked for a single answer for\nsix arms.**  The warning was inlined as a settled premise and violated in the questions below it.\n\n**WHERE THE SEATS AGREE (4 of 5):** the five PURE-STORAGE arms and `LCA_IOC_CONF` must be decided\nseparately, because CONF is the only one with a consumer (`bus = d->ioc_conf & 1`).  For the five,\npremise 6 survives -- grok's formulation is the one to keep: **\"B copies RCR1's *edit* without\nRCR1's *reason*.\"**  RCR1's warrant was that the stored value has a meaning the model otherwise\nhonours; for the five window/TB_ENA fields nothing honours anything, so the edit does not transfer.\nThe kimi substitute sharpens even the agreement: the right analogy for the five is not \"the lie is\npre-existing\" but **`#447`'s ICR cell -- *no honored state to corrupt*** -- which is a stronger and\ndifferently-grounded warrant than the one my premise 6 offered.\n\n**WHERE THEY SPLIT, AND IT IS A REAL DISPUTE TO SETTLE BY MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN BY COUNT:**\n\n    CONF -> B                 glm       \"premise 2 names a live consumer, so CONF is exactly RCR1-shaped\"\n    CONF -> A, more so        minimax   \"the consumer uses only bit 0, so a rejected value leaves it\n                                         correct -- and B can SILENTLY BREAK it if the retained old\n                                         value differs in bit 0\"\n    CONF -> decline or mask   kimi      split per arm\n    CONF -> neither           grok      \"treat it as a bit-mask leftover, not a window\"\n\n**minimax's objection to B is the one with a measurable consequence** and it is the opposite of\nglm's: under B a rejected CONF write leaves the PREVIOUS value in place, and if the previous value\ndiffers in bit 0 the PCI bus selection silently changes -- so B is not merely conservative there, it\ncan alter a consumer that A leaves correct.  That is checkable and should be checked before either\nis adopted.\n\n**THE DISSENT, recorded as a dissent and not as noise:** deepseek alone says premise 6 is unsound\nfor all six -- *\"the guard's accepted-value set is the model's CONTRACT.  Storing a rejected value\nand returning it on readback creates a new false belief that the value is supported, even if\naccepted values are also unimplemented.  The existing lie for accepted values does not license\nexpanding the set of lies.\"*  It also warns the reasoning generalises: it would justify removing any\nstore-before-guard exit anywhere.  **That generalisation objection is the strongest single argument\nagainst A and is not answered by the five-versus-one split**, because it attacks the warrant rather\nthan its scope.\n\n**A BRIEF DEFECT OF MINE, named by glm and correct:** premise 2's field counts were *asserted, not\ninlined* -- I ran the greps and wrote the conclusion instead of the OUTPUT.  This project's own rule\nis that a brief inlines the DERIVER'S OUTPUT rather than a literal, precisely so a packet-fed seat\ncan check it.  glm could not settle five of the six arms from the packet for exactly that reason and\nsaid so honestly rather than ratifying.\n\n**NOT ADOPTED, PENDING codex AND THE ROSTER.**  The next brief is per-arm by construction, carries\nthe deriver output rather than its summary, and puts minimax's silently-broken-consumer prediction\nas a question a measurement can answer.\n\n*** THE grok BODY, READ IN FULL 2026-09-07 -- AND NONE OF THE FOLLOWING WAS IN ITS VERDICT LINE.\nThis is the read-the-body rule paying off on the same day a row was filed about not reading. ***\n\n**1. IT ANSWERS deepseek's DISSENT, which the five/one split could not.**  deepseek's objection was\nthat premise 6 generalises -- it would license removing any store-before-guard exit anywhere.  grok\nscopes it precisely: *\"premise 6 is not the reasoning that lets every store-before-guard site keep\nits store.  It is the reasoning that lets a site keep its store WHEN NO ACCEPTED VALUE OF THAT FIELD\nIS CONSUMED.  That is #447's own per-site rule, not an evasion of it.\"*  The warrant is a per-site\ntest with a checkable predicate, not a blanket.\n\n**2. PREMISE 5 SCORED: \"The comments exist.  The transfer does not.\"**  RCR1 refuses CONSUMED\nFEATURE BITS; these guards whitelist UNIMPLEMENTED CONSTANTS.  That is the distinction the whole\nfork turned on, and it is sharper than my premise 6 offered.\n\n**3. *** OPTION A OPENS A NEW KILL PATH, AND THIS IS THE ROUND'S SAFETY FINDING. ***  Under A, a CONF\nwrite with extra bits that ALSO sets bit 0 reaches `lca_pci_conf`'s `bus != 0` exit(1) -- *\"newly\nreachable ... today that write dies in the CONF guard first.\"*  **A is not purely a reduction; it\nconverts one kill into a different reachable one.**  Any round shipping A must say so, and should\nconsider whether `lca_pci_conf` is in scope with it.\n\n**4. THE FILE'S OWN IDIOM IS A, AND THE GUARDS ARE THE OUTLIERS.**  In the SAME switch:\n`LCA_IOC_BASE` (HAE) and `TBIA` already silently ignore; `W_T_BASE0/1` already accept-and-store; and\nthe `dev >= 21` loop already deletes a kill and substitutes a default.  *\"The value guards are the\noutliers in THIS switch, not the house style.\"*  That is a stronger warrant than anything my brief\noffered, and it is one grep away.\n\n**5. THE ANNOUNCE TEXT WOULD BECOME A WRONG RECORD THE MOMENT A LANDS.**  The current string says\n*\"the only implemented value\"* about a value that **is not implemented**.  The honest once-message is\n*\"window translation / extra CONF bits not implemented\"*, not *\"illegal encoding\"*.  Rewrite it or\ndelete it with the exit -- do not carry it forward.\n\n**6. OPTION-SET CORRECTIONS.**  `#443`'s restore is *\"B wearing extra lines\"* -- observationally B\nfor readback, since nothing re-enters between the store and the `break`; not a distinct member.\n**STAT0 is the architecturally correct error path for LCA** (`IOC_STAT0_ERR` is R/W1C) and is\n**BLOCKED**: STAT0/STAT1 have no case and hit `default:` exit(1) -- *\"You cannot report an error\nthrough a register that currently kills the host.\"*  That member owes a STAT0 implementation first,\nwhich is a different round.  **FAULT confirmed unavailable**: `memory_rw.c` raises on `res <= 0` only\nunder MEM_MIPS and MEM_M88K; Alpha compiles with MEM_ALPHA and has no arm, so `return 0` fails the\naccess with no guest exception.\n\n**7. MASKING CONF IS UNLICENSED, WITH A REASON I DID NOT HAVE: THERE IS NO DATASHEET.**  No 21066\ndocument is in the packet or in the tracked `_scratchpad/*.pdf` list, so RAZ/WI versus store-full is\n**not settleable from this tree**.  Premise 3 stands and masking would be an addition without a\nwarrant.\n\n*** 8. THE WITNESS IS NOT PENDING -- THE RECIPE EXISTS.  This moves the round off documented-only. ***\n* **Machine: `alphabook1` ONLY.**  `machine_alpha.c`'s `device_add(machine, \"lca\")` is under\n  `ST_ALPHABOOK1` and no other subtype.\n* **Addresses** (K0SEG | physical, because `alpha_translate_v2p` identity-maps K0SEG in kernel mode):\n  CONF `0xfffffc0180000020`, TB_ENA `...00a0`, BASE0 `...0100`, BASE1 `...0120`, MASK0 `...0140`,\n  MASK1 `...0160`.\n* **`regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py` ALREADY issues a guest `stq`/`ldq` through this exact decode at\n  CONF on unmodified `alphabook1`**; the other five are further cases of the same DEVICE_ACCESS on\n  the same mapping.\n* **Three traps that file already paid for, so a new probe need not rediscover them:** debugger\n  `dump` skips `DM_DEFAULT` handlers; a debugger `put d` of a killing value IS a host kill but is\n  NOT a guest instruction (so it does not witness); and HAE at window offset 0 silently succeeds, so\n  **a miss control must not use `LCA_IOC_BASE+0`**.\n\n**9. THE KILL CENSUS FOR THE WHOLE FILE:** eleven live `exit(1)` today; **A removes six; five\nremain** -- the `default:` arm (both reads AND writes, covering STAT0/STAT1 and the rest of the\n512 MB IOC window), `lca_pci_conf`'s `bus != 0`, `lca_isa`'s `ofs > len` (filed as [[lcaisalane]]),\nand the bare `lca_interrupt_assert`/`_deassert` TODO pair (member 7, not this round).  Residuals not\nof this class: HAE writes discarded with no storage; TBIA ignored; `default:` still narrows\n`relative_addr` through `(int)` -- **`#506` fixed the `idata` half only**; and [[lcashiftub]].\n\n**10. A CENSUS ERROR IN THIS ROW'S OWN HISTORY:** the guards are TWO predicate shapes, not one --\nbit-masks (CONF, TB_ENA) and equality whitelists (the four windows) -- **and `W_BASE0` accepts TWO\nvalues**, so the row's original \"four registers accept exactly one value each\" framing was false of\n`BASE0` before the title was even corrected to six.  One DESIGN still covers all six (delete the\nexit, keep the store, optional latch); one design does NOT if you pick B, because *\"B is a coherent\nanswer only for a consumed field, which five of these are not.\"*\n\n*** THE codex SEAT LANDED LAST AND OVERTURNED THE PANEL ON A FACT CHECKABLE IN ONE GREP.  MY\nPREMISE 6 IS DEAD, AND SO IS THE FORMULATION I HAD CREDITED grok WITH. ***\n\n**codex's verdict:** *\"Your argument for A fails as written: RCR1 also has no functional consumer.\nI recommend B for pass 1 as a conservative policy choice; hardware-correct write/readback behavior\nremains unproved.\"*\n\n**VERIFIED HERE, not taken on trust** -- `grep -rn rtc_rcr1 src/` returns exactly FOUR occurrences:\n\n    dev_sh4.c:388    uint8_t   rtc_rcr1;              declaration\n    dev_sh4.c:2776   odata = d->rtc_rcr1;             READBACK\n    dev_sh4.c:2781   ...landed in rtc_rcr1 and...     INSIDE THE COMMENT\n    dev_sh4.c:2805   d->rtc_rcr1 = idata;             STORE\n\n**Zero functional consumers.**  And the comparison that settles it: `ioc_conf` also has four\noccurrences, but its fourth is `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` -- a real consumer.  **So RCR1 is consumed\nLESS than LCA's CONF, not more.**\n\n*** WHAT THIS DESTROYS. ***  My premise 6 claimed LCA differs from RCR1 because *\"RCR1's stored value\nhas a meaning the model otherwise honours\"*.  It has no such meaning: the model stores it and reads\nit back, exactly like the five LCA window fields.  **grok's formulation, which I recorded above as\n\"the keeper\" -- *\"RCR1 refuses CONSUMED FEATURE BITS; these guards whitelist UNIMPLEMENTED\nCONSTANTS\"* -- is FALSE IN ITS FIRST HALF.**  So is my own gloss that `#447`'s edit \"does not\ntransfer\".\n\n**IT TRANSFERS EXACTLY.**  `#447` faced a store-before-guard field with NO functional consumer,\nand chose to MOVE THE STORE BELOW THE GUARD.  That is the LCA shape, arm for arm.  The tree's own\nprecedent is therefore evidence FOR B, which is what codex, deepseek and (for CONF) glm all said.\n\n*** AND THE SURVIVING ARGUMENT IS THE ONE deepseek RAISED FIRST AND codex SHARPENED: THE GUEST IS\nITSELF A CONSUMER OF READBACK. ***  codex: *\"Previously, a rejected write ended execution before\nsubsequent guest code could observe that value.  A creates that observation and allows the guest to\nmake further decisions from it.  Existing incomplete behavior for accepted settings does not\nestablish that these newly admitted executions are equally harmless.\"*  That argument is untouched by\nthe consumer census, because it does not depend on a MODEL consumer at all -- and it is precisely\nwhat `#447`'s comment means by *\"the guest writes CIE|AIE, reads CIE|AIE back, and believes the RTC\nwill interrupt it.\"*\n\n**TALLY, and the argument decides it rather than the count:** codex -> B; deepseek -> B; glm -> B for\nCONF and not settleable for the five; kimi substitute -> split per arm; grok -> A; minimax -> A.  But\ngrok's and minimax's A both rest on the consumed/unconsumed distinction, **and that distinction is\nnow measured false.**  Count the arguments, not the seats: the load-bearing premise under the A\nposition has been refuted by a grep.\n\n**THIS IS THE THIRD REVERSAL IN THIS ROUND'S DESIGN AND IT COST NOTHING, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THE\nHOLD DISCIPLINE.**  No code is committed; the round has produced adjudication text and zero product\nchanges.  The earlier records above -- premise 6 \"sound for five arms\", grok's formulation as \"the\nkeeper\" -- are left standing WITH this correction beneath them rather than silently edited, because\na future reader needs to see that the panel's majority reasoning was overturned and by what.\n\n*** WHAT IS NOW OWED BEFORE ANY FIX: the ARM-BY-ARM design, with the RCR1 precedent read as\nSUPPORTING B, and codex's readback-consumer argument answered rather than assumed away.  The\nquestion that remains genuinely open is minimax's measurable one -- under B a rejected CONF write\nretains the previous value, and codex adds that W_MASK0/W_BASE1/W_MASK1 RESET TO ZERO, which is NOT\nin their accepted set, so \"retain the last accepted value\" is not even well-defined at reset.  That\nis a concrete defect in B as stated and it needs an answer (retain? force the reset value? decline\nonly the guarded bits?) before B can ship. ***\n\n*** AND THE SHARPEST LESSON IS NOT THE REVERSAL, IT IS HOW MY GLOSS BEAT MY OWN PRIMARY SOURCE. ***\n\nThe brief inlined #447s RCR1 comment VERBATIM.  That comment argues purely from READBACK:\n\n    the guest writes CIE|AIE, reads CIE|AIE back, and believes the RTC will\n    interrupt it.  It never will.\n\n**It never claims a model consumer.**  The consumed/unconsumed framing is MINE -- premise 6 glossed\nit as RCR1s stored value having a meaning the model otherwise honours -- and grok adopted my\ngloss rather than the quoted text sitting directly above it, producing B copies RCR1s edit\nwithout RCR1s reason.  codex went to the source, grepped the field, and found the gloss false.\n\n> **INLINING THE PRIMARY SOURCE DOES NOT PROTECT A BRIEF FROM A WRONG SUMMARY PLACED BESIDE IT.**\n> A seat reads the summary as the QUESTION and the quote as SUPPORTING MATERIAL, so a false gloss\n> steers the answer even when the refuting text is on the same page.  This projects premise-audit\n> rule says to state load-bearing premises AS QUESTIONS; the refinement is that a premise which\n> RESTATES an inlined quotation is the most dangerous kind, because it looks already verified.\n\n*The cheap defence is the one codex used and I did not: when a premise characterises what some\nother code does, GREP THAT CODE.  `grep -rn rtc_rcr1 src/` is four lines of output and it was\navailable to me before the brief went out.*"
regress/artifacts\lcashiftub\pass2_c7d7414\codex.txt:1312:30860:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, raised by the codex seat (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt) and verified:\n\n    for (dev = 0; dev < 21; dev++)\n            if (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n                    break;\n\n0x800 is a plain int (2^11).  At dev == 20 the expression is 2^31, which overflows a signed 32-bit int --\n**undefined behaviour** in C99, not merely implementation-defined.\n\n**The irony worth recording: this loop is the file's own PRECEDENT for graceful degradation.**  Its\n`dev >= 21' fallback has a commented-out fatal()/exit(1) and executes `dev = 0;' instead -- the\ndelete-the-check-and-substitute-a-safe-default disposition that [[lcabadval]] cites as a transferable\nin-tree warrant.  **The safe path contains UB.**\n\nLOW: `0x800U << dev' fixes it, and on every compiler this project targets the emitted code is already what\nwas intended.  Recorded because a UB shift is exactly what a sanitiser build exists to catch, and\nbuild-asan/ is kept clean -- **so the first question is whether a UBSan sweep already flags it, and if not,\nwhy not.**\n\n*** THE FILED QUESTION IS ANSWERED, AND THE ANSWER COMES WITH A FREE VEHICLE: THE PROBE SHIPPED FOR\n[[lcaconfbus1kill]] ALREADY EVALUATES THIS EXPRESSION ON EVERY RUN. ***\n\nThis row asks *\"whether a UBSan sweep already flags it, and if not, why not.\"*  Both halves settled\nby reading, 2026-09-07.\n\n**UBSAN IS PRESENT.**  `build-asan/Makefile:9` carries\n`-fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g`.\n\n**BUT THE SWEEP CANNOT REACH THIS SITE, AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT.**\n`gate_asan_sweep.sh` runs, per machine type and subtype:\n\n    timeout 25 stdbuf -o0 -e0 \"$bin\" -V -E \"$t\" -e \"$s\" \"$DUMMY\" </dev/null\n\nThat is a **CONSTRUCTION** sweep -- it builds each machine and lets it start.  The shift lives in\n`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`'s device-scan loop, which runs only when a guest performs a PCI\nCONFIG-SPACE ACCESS.  **Constructing `alphabook1` never executes that loop**, so no amount of\nsweeping flags it.  *This is the witness-ladder distinction one level down: rung 2 (construction)\ncannot reach an access-path defect, which is exactly what the ladder says about registration\nproving presence and not reachability.*\n\n*** AND THE VEHICLE ALREADY EXISTS, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH ACTING ON. ***\n`regress/lca_confbus_probe.py` (shipped 2026-09-07 as the witness for [[lcaconfbus1kill]]) drives a\nguest `ldq` into the PCI config window with `ioc_conf == 0`.  That takes the `bus == 0` branch:\n\n    for (dev=0; dev<21; dev++)\n            if (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n                    break;\n\nIts control read uses `relative_addr == 0`, so `tag = (relative_addr >> 5) & ~0xff` is **0**, the\n`if` is never true, and **the loop runs all 21 iterations** -- evaluating `0x800 << 20` at the last\none.  `0x800` is `2^11` and a plain `int`, so `0x800 << 20` is `2^31`: **signed overflow, C99\n6.5.7p4, undefined behaviour, on every single run of that probe.**\n\n> **SO THE MEASUREMENT IS: run the existing `lca_confbus_probe.py` against `build-asan/gxemul` and\n> read stderr for a UBSAN shift diagnostic.  No new probe, no new row, no new apparatus.**  The\n> expected diagnostic names `dev_lca.c` and a left-shift of a signed value.\n\n**NOT RUN YET** -- a probe run must not overlap the codex census currently in flight.  Queued.\n\n**IF IT FIRES**, this row gains a measured witness and its one-token repair (`0x800U << dev`) can be\ngraded properly.  **IF IT DOES NOT**, that is itself a finding about\n[[ubsanshiftlane]] -- it would mean the ASAN build is not reporting a shift UB that is provably\nevaluated, and the whole proposed UBSAN differential lane rests on an assumption that does not hold.\n**Either outcome is worth the one run**, which is the argument for doing it before designing the lane.\n\n*Found while answering this row's own filed question rather than by looking for it -- and note the\nirony the row already recorded: this loop is the file's OWN graceful-degradation precedent, cited by\n[[lcabadval]] as a transferable warrant.  The safe path contains the UB.*\n\n*** MEASURED 2026-09-07.  THE UBSAN DIAGNOSTIC FIRES, NAMING THE FILE, LINE, COLUMN, VALUE AND\nSHIFT COUNT.  Artifact: `regress/artifacts/lcashiftub/ubsan_HEAD_20260907.raw`. ***\n\n    GXemul> step 5\n    fffffc0001040000:  243f0102    ldah  t0,0x102\n    fffffc0001040004:  a4410010    ldq   t1,16(t0)\n    fffffc0001040008:  a4810000    ldq   t3,0(t0)\n    fffffc000104000c:  a4210008    ldq   t0,8(t0)\n    fffffc0001040010:  a4620000    ldq   t2,0(t1)\n    dev_lca.c:125:21: runtime error: left shift of 2048 by 20 places cannot be\n                      represented in type 'int'\n    GXemul> print t2\n    0xffffffffffffffff\n\n**`2048` is `0x800`; `by 20 places` is the final loop iteration; `dev_lca.c:125` is exactly\n`if (tag & (0x800 << dev))`.**  The diagnostic lands on the guest `ldq t2,0(t1)` -- a real guest\ninstruction through real address decode on an unmodified `alphabook1`, with the empty-slot signature\n`0xffffffffffffffff` printed immediately after.\n\n**NO NEW APPARATUS WAS BUILT.**  This is `regress/lca_confbus_probe.py`, shipped earlier the same day\nas the witness for [[lcaconfbus1kill]], run against `build-asan/gxemul` instead of `build/gxemul`.\nThe control read takes the `bus == 0` branch with `tag == 0`, so the scan loop runs all 21\niterations and reaches the overflow every time.\n\n*** AND THE FIRST MEASUREMENT WAS A FALSE NEGATIVE OF MY OWN MAKING, WHICH IS WORTH MORE THAN THE\nRESULT. ***  Run 1 reported NO diagnostic and I nearly recorded that as a finding against\n[[ubsanshiftlane]].  The cause: **I grepped the PROBE's stdout, and a UBSAN message goes to the\nEMULATOR's stderr**, which lands in the probe's internal pty buffer and is never echoed.  That is\n*exactly* the defect I had fixed hours earlier for the `bus attrib` row -- a green row whose evidence\nnever reaches the artifact -- recurring one level out, on the harness rather than the row.\n\n> **A PROBE ONLY SHOWS YOU WHAT IT CHOOSES TO PRINT.  When you are looking for output the probe was\n> not written to surface, dump the buffer -- do not grep the summary.**  Re-run from a frozen COPY\n> in `/tmp` (the shipped probe is not edited for a measurement) with the full buffer echoed, and the\n> diagnostic was in the first ten lines.\n\n*The wrong answer REASSURED, which is this project's recurring signature: \"no diagnostic\" is exactly\nwhat a clean build looks like.*\n\n**THIS ROW NOW HAS A MEASURED WITNESS** and its one-token repair (`0x800U << dev`) is gradeable.\nThe repair is COMPLETE for this site: `0x800U` makes the operand unsigned, so `2048U << 20` is\n`0x80000000U`, well inside `unsigned int`, and the `tag &` comparison is unaffected because `tag` is\nalready a wider unsigned quantity.\n\n**SEVERITY STAYS LOW and that is deliberate**: the overflow is UB by the language, the emitted code\non every compiler this project targets is what was intended, and no wrong guest-visible value has\nbeen measured.  What changed is that it is now WITNESSED rather than argued.\n\n*** THE FIX IS DESIGNED AND EXECUTABLE.  Written out here so the next session can run it rather than\nre-derive it -- the round is small and every input is now in hand. ***\n\n**THE EDIT, one token:**\n\n    -\tif (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n    +\tif (tag & (0x800U << dev))\t/*  #NNN  */\n\n`0x800U` makes the operand `unsigned int`, so `2048U << 20` is `0x80000000U` -- well inside\n`unsigned int` -- and no other behaviour moves: `tag` is already a wider unsigned quantity, so the\n`&` and the truth value are unchanged at every one of the 21 iterations.  **The emitted code on\nevery compiler this project targets is what was already intended**, which is why the severity is LOW\neven though the language calls it undefined.\n\n*** THE TEST-FIRST ORDER IS ALREADY SATISFIED, WHICH IS UNUSUAL AND WORTH NOTING. ***  The witness\nexists and is RED on the committed build: running `regress/lca_confbus_probe.py` against\n`build-asan/gxemul` emits\n\n    dev_lca.c:125:21: runtime error: left shift of 2048 by 20 places cannot be\n                      represented in type 'int'\n\nSo the reproduce-before-you-edit step is DONE and preserved\n(`regress/artifacts/lcashiftub/ubsan_HEAD_20260907.raw`).  The post-fix half is: the same command,\nsame binary path, and **the diagnostic must be ABSENT**.  Same script, opposite outcome -- which is\nthe ladder's clause (i) exactly.\n\n**WHAT THE ROUND OWES, and the one real decision in it:**\n\nThe two-direction control above is a UBSAN DIFFERENTIAL, and this project has that filed as its own\nround ([[ubsanshiftlane]]) because it is a NEW MEASUREMENT LANE -- a second binary and a STDERR\noracle rather than a value oracle.  **So the honest choice is:**\n\n* **(a) ship the fix with the UBSAN differential as its detector**, which means building at least a\n  minimal version of the lane in this round -- and a lane added inside a fix round is a lane nobody\n  reviewed, which is the exact objection `ubsanshiftlane` was filed to avoid; or\n* **(b) ship the fix with a SOURCE PIN** -- a check asserting the expression is `0x800U` -- and\n  leave the differential to its own round.  `#510`'s own product comments already name a source pin\n  as the legitimate alternative to UBSAN for precisely this class (\"No row can kill a `1U' -> `1'\n  regression; UBSAN or a source pin can\").\n\n**(b) is in scope and (a) is not**, on this project's own stopping rule: the fix is a one-token\ncorrectness repair, and expanding it into a new measurement lane is the scope creep the rule exists\nto refuse.  *Recorded as a reasoned choice rather than a preference, so a reviewer can disagree with\nthe reason.*\n\n**COST: a build.**  The edit must propagate to all four trees and `build-asan` must be REBUILT for\nthe post-fix measurement, which is the only expensive part and is why this was not executed the\nmoment it was designed -- a rebuild must not overlap a running seat.\n\n**AND THE POST-FIX RUN IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE FIX.**  If the diagnostic does NOT\ndisappear, the repair is wrong or the site is not the one being reported, and that is a finding\nabout the measurement rather than a formality.  Predict-then-measure: it should vanish, and nothing\nelse in the probe's four rows should move (3/4 before, 3/4 after -- the `bus kill` row is a\ndifferent defect and stays RED).\n\n*** FIXED AND MEASURED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, 2026-09-07, as `#511`. ***\n\n    HEAD    1 diagnostic     ubsan_HEAD_20260907.raw\n    FIXED   0 diagnostics    ubsan_FIXED_20260907.raw\n    control  build/ scores 3/4 before AND after, same three rows green, `bus kill' still RED\n             (a different defect) -- NOTHING ELSE MOVED\n\n**The pre-registered prediction held exactly**: the diagnostic vanished and no other row moved.  All\nfour trees cleared two-part after the propagate (`est/`: source tree, part 2 N/A).\n\n**The detector is the differential itself and it is NOT GATE-WIRED**, which the CHANGELOG block says\nin those words.  A source pin was considered and rejected: it would test the TEXT while this round\nhas the BEHAVIOUR.  Gate-wiring is [[ubsanshiftlane]], deliberately separate.\n\n*** THE SIBLING QUESTION IS ANSWERED BEFORE THE PANEL ANSWERS IT, AND THE RESULT IS A CLEAN\nABSENCE -- RECORDED WITH ITS WARRANT SO IT IS FALSIFIABLE. ***\n\n`#511`'s own pass-2 brief asks (Q5) whether a sibling of this shape was missed.  Censused:\n\n    signed-constant  <<  VARIABLE, tree-wide                       317 sites\n    of those, literal > 1 AND headroom <= 24                          2 sites\n\n**317 IS A CORRELATE, NOT THE CLASS.**  `1 << x` with 31 places of headroom is the ordinary bitmask\nidiom in C and is a defect only if `x` can reach 31.  The `#511` SHAPE is distinctive: a literal\nLARGER than 1 (so little headroom) shifted by a variable that a loop can drive to the overflow point.\nFiltering on that leaves TWO:\n\n    cpu_mips_coproc.c:772   tmp &= 0x0fff << PAGEMASK_SHIFT;    headroom 20\n    cpu_mips_coproc.c:774   tmp &= 0xffff << PAGEMASK_SHIFT;    headroom 16\n\n**BOTH ARE SAFE, and the warrant is a compile-time constant:**\n\n    src/include/cop0.h:85    #define PAGEMASK_SHIFT   13\n    0x0fff << 13 = 0x01ffe000       0xffff << 13 = 0x1fffe000       INT_MAX = 0x7fffffff\n\n`PAGEMASK_SHIFT` is a MACRO, not a runtime variable, so the compiler folds the whole expression and\nthere is no shift to overflow.  **My regex matched an identifier and could not tell a macro from a\nvariable** -- which is why the two hits needed reading rather than counting.\n\n> **ABSENCE RECORD, with its warrant named so a later edit can invalidate it: no other site in the\n> tree has a signed literal > 1 shifted by a value that can reach 2^31.  THE WARRANT IS\n> `PAGEMASK_SHIFT == 13` AND ITS BEING A COMPILE-TIME CONSTANT.**  If either changes -- the value\n> raised past 15, or the macro replaced by a variable -- the two `cpu_mips_coproc.c` sites become\n> live and this record expires.\n\n*A near-miss worth keeping: my first attempt grepped `define PAGEMASK_SHIFT` in `src/` and returned\nNOTHING, and I had already computed the arithmetic assuming 13.  Zero hits is not evidence, and the\nnumber I was about to rely on was a guess.  It IS 13 -- found in `src/include/cop0.h` on a broader\nsearch -- but the conclusion was reached before the fact was.*",
regress/artifacts\lcashiftub\pass2_c7d7414\codex.txt:1315:31440:   "state_note": "Filed 2026-09-07 from `#510`'s pass 2.  Full record in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md under the same id.\n\ngrok showed two one-line regressions that re-open real UB and score FULL MARKS on a 12-row guest-value probe, and the reason is STRUCTURAL: both are DEFINEDNESS repairs, and definedness has no guest-visible value on a non-trapping host.  `1 << 31' and `1U << 31' give the same mask after the `- 1'; with a correct 32-bit mask `x |= ~mask' is `x |= 0'.\n\n**build-asan already carries `-fsanitize=address,undefined`** -- established AFTER I wrote \"unchecked\" about it in the probe header, one grep from the answer.  So the class IS pinnable, as a DIFFERENTIAL: fixed source produces no shift diagnostic, the `1U'->`1' mutant produces one.\n\n**Not bolted onto #510**: a second binary and a STDERR oracle is a new measurement lane, and #510's pass 2 had already run.  A lane added after a pass is a lane nobody reviewed.\n\nCovers the same species at [[lcashiftub]], [[sgirebufshiftub]], m88k_rot:513, cpu_mips_instr.c:1757 and the two is_userpage sites -- EIGHT sites across three CPUs, none directly visible to a value oracle.\n\n*** THE FILED QUESTION IS ANSWERED, AND THE ANSWER COMES WITH A FREE VEHICLE: THE PROBE SHIPPED FOR\n[[lcaconfbus1kill]] ALREADY EVALUATES THIS EXPRESSION ON EVERY RUN. ***\n\nThis row asks *\"whether a UBSan sweep already flags it, and if not, why not.\"*  Both halves settled\nby reading, 2026-09-07.\n\n**UBSAN IS PRESENT.**  `build-asan/Makefile:9` carries\n`-fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g`.\n\n**BUT THE SWEEP CANNOT REACH THIS SITE, AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT.**\n`gate_asan_sweep.sh` runs, per machine type and subtype:\n\n    timeout 25 stdbuf -o0 -e0 \"$bin\" -V -E \"$t\" -e \"$s\" \"$DUMMY\" </dev/null\n\nThat is a **CONSTRUCTION** sweep -- it builds each machine and lets it start.  The shift lives in\n`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`'s device-scan loop, which runs only when a guest performs a PCI\nCONFIG-SPACE ACCESS.  **Constructing `alphabook1` never executes that loop**, so no amount of\nsweeping flags it.  *This is the witness-ladder distinction one level down: rung 2 (construction)\ncannot reach an access-path defect, which is exactly what the ladder says about registration\nproving presence and not reachability.*\n\n*** AND THE VEHICLE ALREADY EXISTS, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH ACTING ON. ***\n`regress/lca_confbus_probe.py` (shipped 2026-09-07 as the witness for [[lcaconfbus1kill]]) drives a\nguest `ldq` into the PCI config window with `ioc_conf == 0`.  That takes the `bus == 0` branch:\n\n    for (dev=0; dev<21; dev++)\n            if (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n                    break;\n\nIts control read uses `relative_addr == 0`, so `tag = (relative_addr >> 5) & ~0xff` is **0**, the\n`if` is never true, and **the loop runs all 21 iterations** -- evaluating `0x800 << 20` at the last\none.  `0x800` is `2^11` and a plain `int`, so `0x800 << 20` is `2^31`: **signed overflow, C99\n6.5.7p4, undefined behaviour, on every single run of that probe.**\n\n> **SO THE MEASUREMENT IS: run the existing `lca_confbus_probe.py` against `build-asan/gxemul` and\n> read stderr for a UBSAN shift diagnostic.  No new probe, no new row, no new apparatus.**  The\n> expected diagnostic names `dev_lca.c` and a left-shift of a signed value.\n\n**NOT RUN YET** -- a probe run must not overlap the codex census currently in flight.  Queued.\n\n**IF IT FIRES**, this row gains a measured witness and its one-token repair (`0x800U << dev`) can be\ngraded properly.  **IF IT DOES NOT**, that is itself a finding about\n[[ubsanshiftlane]] -- it would mean the ASAN build is not reporting a shift UB that is provably\nevaluated, and the whole proposed UBSAN differential lane rests on an assumption that does not hold.\n**Either outcome is worth the one run**, which is the argument for doing it before designing the lane.\n\n*Found while answering this row's own filed question rather than by looking for it -- and note the\nirony the row already recorded: this loop is the file's OWN graceful-degradation precedent, cited by\n[[lcabadval]] as a transferable warrant.  The safe path contains the UB.*\n\n*** MEASURED 2026-09-07.  THE UBSAN DIAGNOSTIC FIRES, NAMING THE FILE, LINE, COLUMN, VALUE AND\nSHIFT COUNT.  Artifact: `regress/artifacts/lcashiftub/ubsan_HEAD_20260907.raw`. ***\n\n    GXemul> step 5\n    fffffc0001040000:  243f0102    ldah  t0,0x102\n    fffffc0001040004:  a4410010    ldq   t1,16(t0)\n    fffffc0001040008:  a4810000    ldq   t3,0(t0)\n    fffffc000104000c:  a4210008    ldq   t0,8(t0)\n    fffffc0001040010:  a4620000    ldq   t2,0(t1)\n    dev_lca.c:125:21: runtime error: left shift of 2048 by 20 places cannot be\n                      represented in type 'int'\n    GXemul> print t2\n    0xffffffffffffffff\n\n**`2048` is `0x800`; `by 20 places` is the final loop iteration; `dev_lca.c:125` is exactly\n`if (tag & (0x800 << dev))`.**  The diagnostic lands on the guest `ldq t2,0(t1)` -- a real guest\ninstruction through real address decode on an unmodified `alphabook1`, with the empty-slot signature\n`0xffffffffffffffff` printed immediately after.\n\n**NO NEW APPARATUS WAS BUILT.**  This is `regress/lca_confbus_probe.py`, shipped earlier the same day\nas the witness for [[lcaconfbus1kill]], run against `build-asan/gxemul` instead of `build/gxemul`.\nThe control read takes the `bus == 0` branch with `tag == 0`, so the scan loop runs all 21\niterations and reaches the overflow every time.\n\n*** AND THE FIRST MEASUREMENT WAS A FALSE NEGATIVE OF MY OWN MAKING, WHICH IS WORTH MORE THAN THE\nRESULT. ***  Run 1 reported NO diagnostic and I nearly recorded that as a finding against\n[[ubsanshiftlane]].  The cause: **I grepped the PROBE's stdout, and a UBSAN message goes to the\nEMULATOR's stderr**, which lands in the probe's internal pty buffer and is never echoed.  That is\n*exactly* the defect I had fixed hours earlier for the `bus attrib` row -- a green row whose evidence\nnever reaches the artifact -- recurring one level out, on the harness rather than the row.\n\n> **A PROBE ONLY SHOWS YOU WHAT IT CHOOSES TO PRINT.  When you are looking for output the probe was\n> not written to surface, dump the buffer -- do not grep the summary.**  Re-run from a frozen COPY\n> in `/tmp` (the shipped probe is not edited for a measurement) with the full buffer echoed, and the\n> diagnostic was in the first ten lines.\n\n*The wrong answer REASSURED, which is this project's recurring signature: \"no diagnostic\" is exactly\nwhat a clean build looks like.*\n\n*** THE LANE'S PREMISE IS CONFIRMED BY MEASUREMENT: THE ASAN BUILD DOES REPORT SHIFT UB AT RUNTIME,\nWITH FILE, LINE AND COLUMN. ***  This row proposed a UBSAN DIFFERENTIAL to pin the shift-UB class\nthat no guest-value row can see, and rested on the untested assumption that `build-asan` would\nactually emit such a diagnostic.  **It does**, and the message is precise enough to key an oracle on\n(`dev_lca.c:125:21`, the value, the shift count, and the type).\n\n**So the differential shape is viable as filed:** fixed source emits no diagnostic for that site, the\n`1U`->`1` mutant emits one.  What this measurement does NOT establish is the other half -- that the\ndiagnostic DISAPPEARS once the site is repaired -- which needs the repaired build and is the\ntwo-direction control the lane owes.\n\n*One incidental observation, recorded and NOT filed: the same run ends with `LeakSanitizer: detected\nmemory leaks -- 3211582 byte(s) leaked in 21 allocation(s)`.  The session ends via `exit(1)` from the\nkill under test, so leaks at abrupt termination are expected and this is not evidence of a defect.*"
