1, and \"vectors 11..113\" double-counts them. The comment is then WRONG in its residual enumeration (should start at 13, and should include 2 and 3).\n\nAlternatively, if the four = {0 (RESET), 1 (INTERRUPT?), 2?, 3?}... hmm.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me look at the probe again: `selftest (PSR at reset == 0x80000002)`. Not helpful.\n\nLet me think about \"the four this fallback never covered\" differently. The four marked implemented are vectors that \"this fallback never covered\" \u2014 i.e., for which the fallback's stop-all never fired. If switch 1 marked RESET/INTERRUPT/SFU1_PRECISE/SFU1_IMPRECISE implemented = true, then the fallback never covered them. The seven new ones were \"covered\" by the fallback (stop-all) before. So \"the four\" = the originally-implemented four. The diff comment: \"the seven trap-like classes above are DELIVERED (...), like the four this fallback never covered\" \u2014 the four are delivered (presumably: reset via tail RESET arm [though that arm dies for trap-named 0...], interrupt via tail arm?, SFU1 via... hmm, but SFU1 \"still lands here\" per the same comment!).\n\nContradiction within the comment itself: SFU1 can't be both \"delivered like the four\" and \"still lands here\" (stops). Unless the four = {0, 1, 2?, 3?} hmm.\n\nHmm, wait, actually re-read: \"the seven trap-like classes above are DELIVERED (VBR + 8*vector, SXIP = the faulting instruction, SNIP = the next one, destination register unchanged -- MC88100 UM 6.4.3/6.5/6.6/6.8.3), like the four this fallback never covered.\"\n\nHmm \u2014 \"like the four this fallback never covered\" \u2014 the seven are delivered LIKE the four. So the four are delivered too (they never hit the fallback, and presumably their tail arms or the common path deliver them). If SFU1 stops at the tail default (#if 0'd SFU1 block), then SFU1 is NOT delivered... so SFU1 shouldn't be among \"the four delivered\". But then which four?\n\nPossibility: the four = RESET(0), INTERRUPT(1), and... hmm, we need four. What about vector 114/115? \"USER_TRAPS_START\"? Hmm! Maybe the tail switch has arms for user traps (114..115?) \u2014 the comment's residual \"116..127\" suggests 114 and 115 are handled! So maybe: switch 1 marks implemented: RESET(0), INTERRUPT(1)?, SFU1_PRECISE(11), SFU1_IMPRECISE(12) \u2014 four. Tail arms: RESET, INTERRUPT?, SFU1 block (#if 0), user traps 114/115?, default. Hmm.\n\nHmm, actually \"116..127\" \u2014 why would 114/115 be special? In MC88100 UM: vectors 114-127 are \"user-defined\"? Hmm, or maybe the fork's code has M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAPS_START = 114 and the tail has `case M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAP_1: case ...TRAP_2:`? Hmm.\n\nHmm, hmm. Actually! Wait. Maybe USER_TRAPS_START = 114 and the fallback condition `vector < M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAPS_START` stops vectors < 114. Then the residual (post-diff) hitting the fallback = unimplemented vectors < 114 = {2,3,13..113} = 103 vectors. And \"11..113\" \u2014 hmm still doesn't fit if 11/12 implemented.\n\nUgh, I can't fully settle which four are implemented in switch 1 \u2014 the packet never says. This is a premise I should name: \"which four vectors does switch 1 mark implemented\" \u2014 settle with `grep -n \"implemented = true\" src/cpus/cpu_m88k.c` or reading the first switch in m88k_exception.\n\nBut regardless, the residual enumeration \"11..113/116..127\" has problems I can REASON about:\n\nCase A: SFU1 = 11/12 are implemented in switch 1 (consistent with \"SFU1_PRECISE/IMPRECISE untouched\" in the diff \u2014 the diff says the seven-arm change applies to seven arms and SFU1 untouched \u2014 untouched implies they HAVE arms in switch 1, i.e., marked implemented). Then:\n- \"What still lands here: SFU1_PRECISE/IMPRECISE\" \u2014 TRUE in the sense that they still stop THIS cpu at the tail default (the SFU1 tail block is #if 0), but the parenthetical \"they need the FP exception registers the fork does not write\" is consistent. But \"vectors 11..113\" includes 11/12 \u2192 overlap/redundancy