regress/artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\kimi.txt:596:  - PSR OR-in constant mutants: each bit measured (M_NOSFRZ 2/23, M_NOIND, M_NOSFD1 1/29, M_NOMODE 19/23, carry ovfc). What about OR-in ALSO clearing MXM (`& ~MXM`)? Pre-fault PSRs in rows: 0x80000002, 0x00000002, 0x80000000, 0x90000002, and handler-stcr values 0x80000003/0x00000003. MXM never set in any row (stcr with MXM set stops the cpu — so no row CAN set MXM pre-fault). A mutant `(... | SFRZ|IND|SFD1|MODE) & ~MXM` — differs only when pre-fault MXM set; MXM-set is unreachable in the harness (stcr stops). Also real guests: MXM set + exception -> MXM cleared — behavioral difference on real-hardware-reachable input, but harness-unreachable. Is it in the enumeration? "operand swaps only fields the guarded bodies read" — the body reads cr[PSR] wholesale. Adding `& ~MXM` is an added-mask mutant, like adding a conjunct — the M_OVFSUPONLY family by shape (a condition/mask on a field the body reads). Hmm. But every row's PSR compare would pass (MXM never set). Clean sweep!! Wait — really? `(cr[PSR] | 0xb | 0x80000000) & ~4`: for all rows, cr[PSR] has MXM=0 -> result unchanged. Yes, clean sweep. But is this within ANY plausible reading of the enumeration? It's not a predicate mutation, not an operand swap (no operands swapped), not a joint cell. It's an ADDED operation. The (d) enumeration is about DEPENDS-ON: predicates dominating edited lines + operand swaps of read fields. An added mask is a new operation, not a mutation of an enumerated element — outside (d). (By that logic, though, is M_OVFSUPONLY's added MODE-read inside (d)? It modified the `implemented` assignment — an enumerated element (switch-1 arm body). `& ~MXM` modifies the PSR-write expression — the OR-in IS the #502-edited line? The PSR OR-in line 827-832 — is that edited by #502? It's premised as "The exception path's PSR write is ONE site, an OR-in" — and M_NOSFRZ etc. were measured against it, so the chain treats mutations of that expression as in-scope. Dropping a measured bit is caught; adding a mask on an unmeasured bit (MXM, BO, C...) — C is measured (ovfc: C survives). BO: byte-order flip stops the cpu via stcr (premise 4) — wait, the exception-path PSR write goes THROUGH m88k_stcr (line 827: m88k_stcr(cpu, ... | MODE, M88K_CR_PSR, 0)). stcr checks BO consistency: value's BO must equal cpu's. A mutant `& ~M88K_PSR_BO`... on a big-endian cpu, pre-fault BO=0, fine, no difference. MXM: `& ~MXM` no harness difference. These added-mask mutants: are they "operand swaps"? No. I'd rule them outside (d) as stated — the enumeration covers predicates + swaps, not additive mutations. And M_OVFSUPONLY itself was graded clause-(e) by one seat (not floor) — the additive family is exactly the contested clause-(e) territory. Consistent ruling: additive-condition/mask mutants are clause-(e), and the chain's answer to clause-(e) on the CLASS arms was to add rows (cheap). For the fallback predicate, (A) is additive in the same sense. Hmm — so (A) is clause-(e) by shape (additive conjunct), and the chain's PRECEDENT for exactly this shape was to REPAIR (uovf/umisalign rows), not disclose.
