5's matrix), the implementer's reviewer and the closer overlap.  Disclosed rather than implied.  FILED, not closed here: `Mq_pktretry` escaped `#480`'s table honestly and is recorded as such.  *** AND ONE ELEMENT OF THE ORIGINAL RECOMMENDATION IS SUPERSEDED, NOT PRESERVED. ***  It said to record the ALT-CTRL gap as a deliberately RED row. `#481` CLOSED the gap instead, which is better, and the adjudicator ruled against its own disposition on the reasoning that a permanently red row is the dual of a check-that-cannot-fail -- **a check that cannot PASS** -- and violates the blanks rule, since a deliberate red and a regression red render identically.  Measured twice this week: a battery red went unread for five days over five further defects, and a stuck HELD count read as 'stable' across two regenerations."
regress/artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:5520:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:24282:   "note": "*** MY OWN BRIEF'S PREMISE WAS THAT `gdromcondlies` HELD THE LAST LIVE GUEST-REACHABLE HOST KILLS.  IT IS FALSE, AND THE FIRST FILE I SAMPLED REFUTED IT. ***  I stated it AS A QUESTION in that brief (*'have I confused \"the queue knows of no others\" with \"there are no others\"?'*), which is the only reason this was cheap to find -- and I found it myself, before the panel answered.  **THE CENSUS SAID WHERE TO LOOK AND WAS RIGHT.**  `exit_census.py` reports 99 live terminating calls inside `DEVICE_ACCESS` across 30 files; `dev_sgi_re.c` carries **21**, seven times the gdrom file.  It is the top of that list, and I sampled it for exactly that reason.  *** WHAT IS THERE, AND IT IS A CLASS THIS PROJECT HAS ALREADY FIXED ELSEWHERE: ***  the guard at the head of the CRIME_RE_TLB_A arm is  `if (len != 8) { fatal(\"TODO: unimplemented len=%i ...\"); exit(1); }`  -- **a GUEST CHOOSING AN ACCESS WIDTH ends the host process.**  That is the `#337` class verbatim, whose own comment in `dev_wdc.c` reads *'a 3- or 8-byte access to the data port used to call exit(1) -- a GUEST choosing an access width killed the host process'*.  The adjacent arm kills on a plain READ (`fatal(\"TODO: read from CRIME_RE_TLB_A\"); exit(1);`), which is the direction/offset class.  **REACHABILITY CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED**: `machine_sgi.c:500` calls `dev_sgi_re_init(machine, mem, 0x15000000)` and the device registers at `baseaddr + 0x1000` through `memory_device_register`.  It is a real device on a real machine, mapped into the guest address space.  **HONEST BOUND ON THE NUMBER, because this project keeps getting counts wrong: 21 is how many live terminating calls sit inside that file's `DEVICE_ACCESS` handlers.**  It is NOT a claim that 21 are individually reachable -- some may be guarded, and `dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:1055` is the standing proof that a `DEVICE_ACCESS` site can be unreachable and take a hand-derived warrant to establish it.  What IS established: at least two of them are reachable by construction, and they are of classes already recognised as defects here.  *** THE GENERAL LESSON, AND IT IS ABOUT THE QUEUE RATHER THAN THIS DEVICE: A QUEUE-COMPLETE CLAIM IS NOT A TREE-COMPLETE ONE. ***  The adjudicating seat flagged exactly this limit about its own answer hours earlier -- *'my no-other-live-host-kills claim rests on the queue and this file's grep, not a fresh tree-wide exit census'* -- and then I wrote the queue-complete version into a brief as a premise anyway.  The seat named the gap; the brief inherited it.  SCOPE: this is a CLUSTER and must not become one round.  The round-scope rule takes ONE site, with a second joining only if it is witnessed, in the same device and semantic class, and covered by the same detector with no new oracle.  The width guards may qualify as one class; the read-direction kills are a second.  **More witnesses create more candidate ROUNDS, never bigger rounds.**  NEXT STEP is a rung-3 witness on ONE site, not a fix: `-E sgi` machines exist in tree, the device is mapped at a known base, and the `#337` precedent gives the fix shape (latched decline, `(once)` marker) if a witness lands.  *** THE DIAGNOSIS ABOVE IS INCOMPLETE AND THE TRUE ONE IS WORSE.  Corrected the same day by the opus5 seat, which MEASURED it. ***  This row was filed saying the brief had confused 'the queue knows of no others' with 'there are no others'.  **Neither.  The queue knew of NINE others.**  Ledger row `n6` is OPEN, titled *'The record says the footbridge host-halts were made graceful; nine of them are not'*, carrying a CONFIRMED rung-3 witness (`probe:regress/footbridge_sites_probe.py` row K1, gated in `gate_arm.sh`) and a `state_note` RANKING IT 6th OF 8 on 2026-08-27 -- three days before the brief claimed there were none.  The seat rebuilt HEAD clean and re-ran the COMMITTED probe: a guest `ldr r7,[r0,#392]` on an unmodified `-E cats` ends the host at `dev_footbridge.c:589`.  It then swapped the single planted word three more times and got **four of four** -- `:604` ENABLE_CLEAR read, `:565` IRQ_STATUS write, `:712` SDRAM_BA_MASK read -- each one guest instruction, each with the site's own diagnostic text as the attribution control.  **So this is the UNREAD-ROW class, not a census gap.**  The census work still stands and still found two REAL clusters nobody had filed; what it did not do is what a `grep n6` would have done in one second.  *The lesson is not 'census harder'.  It is that a completeness claim must be checked against THE QUEUE'S OWN OPEN ROWS before it is written down -- and that is the cheaper check of the two.*  *** THE CENSUS IS DONE, AND IT SPLITS THE 21 INTO FOUR CLASSES WITH ONE PROVABLY DEAD SITE.  agy seat, 2026-08-30, `regress/artifacts/widthkillclass/witness_20260830/`. ***  It is a READING, not a measurement, and is recorded as one -- but the single most surprising claim in it was CHECKED HERE BY OPENING THE FILE, and it held.  **20 of 21 guest-reachable; 1 PROVABLY UNREACHABLE.**  WIDTH (a guest choosing an access size ends the host) -- 7 reachable: the `len != 8` guards on CRIME_RE_TLB_A/B/C, CRIME_RE_TEX and CRIME_RE_LINEAR_A/B, plus `sgi_de: len = %i not implemented` (which wants len 4 or 8).  DIRECTION (a legal-width access in the wrong direction) -- 6 reachable: `TODO: read from CRIME_RE_TLB_A/B/C`, `..._TEX`, `..._LINEAR_A/B`, each reached by an 8-byte READ, i.e. **the guest does everything right except the direction**.  BADOFFSET -- 1: the `unimplemented read/write to address` arm, reached by any access to an unregistered sub-window.  BADVAL -- 6: `sgi_de: UNIMPLEMENTED drawing op`, and five `sgi_mte` arms (MTE_MODE_STIPPLE, unimplemented SRC, MTE_MODE_COPY, MTE_DEPTH_x, and an unimplemented bytemask).  *** THE UNREACHABLE ONE IS DEAD CODE AND MUST NOT BE 'FIXED' -- I VERIFIED THIS BY OPENING THE FILE RATHER THAN ACCEPTING IT. ***  `[ sgi_mte: UNIMPLEMENTED read/write len %i ]` sits in the trailing `else` of `if (len == 4) { ... } else if (len != 4) { ... } else { fatal; exit(1); }`.  For every integer `len` one of the first two arms is taken, so the third can never execute.  A round that 'de-fatalises' it would be editing unreachable code and its detector could carry no row -- **the vacuous shape in its purest form, and the census is what stops it being attempted.**  **WHAT THIS MEANS FOR SCOPE, and it is the useful part: FOUR classes means at least four candidate rounds, not one.**  The round-scope rule takes ONE site, with a second joining only if it is the same device AND the same semantic class AND covered by the same detector with no new oracle.  A WIDTH guard and a DIRECTION guard sit in the SAME ARM at the same offset and are still different classes -- whether they nonetheless share one fix design is an open question this row does not settle, and it is exactly what the next research pass should answer rather than assume.  HONEST BOUND: reachability here is derived from address-decode reading, NOT measured, except for the WIDTH class -- which now has a rung-3 witness (see `widthkillclass`).  The DIRECTION, BADOFFSET and BADVAL classes have NO witness yet and a round on any of them owes one first.  *** THE COUNT'S SCOPE WAS WRONG AND A SEAT CHECKED IT: 21 IS THE FILE TOTAL, NOT THE HANDLER'S. ***  `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)` holds **13**.  Per handler: sgi_re 13, sgi_de 1, sgi_mte 1, sgi_de_status 0, helpers 6 -- summing to the 21 this row filed.  So the four-class census spans FOUR HANDLERS, not one, and that matters for scope rather than for tidiness: a round joining sites 'in the same handler' is a different population from one joining sites 'in the same file'.  **This is the dropped-qualifier class again** -- the number was right and the noun was wrong, which is exactly how `b120r`'s NIL and `#464`'s 'floods identically' went wrong.  AND THE WIDTH FIGURE OF 7 IS SHORT BY ONE against the message text: there are EIGHT width-shaped kills in the file, the six in sgi_re plus `sgi_de: len = %i not implemented` and `[ sgi_mte: UNIMPLEMENTED read/write len %i ]`.  Both are now filed separately -- `sgidewidth` (LIVE, high) and `sgimtewidth` (a mis-service plus a resurrection trap) -- because they sit in different handlers and so cannot join a sgi_re round.  *** READ-ONLY AUDIT, agy seat, 2026-08-30 -- AND THE HEADLINE IS THAT THIS ROUND MAY BE MUCH SMALLER THAN IT LOOKS: ALL SIX ARMS LOOK LIKE **ACCEPT**, NOT DECLINE. ***  The de-fatalisation option set has four members, and the fourth applies where the model already holds the right answer and merely discards it.  **`re_tlb_a`, `re_tlb_b`, `re_tlb_c`, `re_tex`, `re_linear_a` and `re_linear_b` are all WRITTEN by the store path**, so a read can return what was written -- no latch, no message, because there is no event to announce.  *A bounded-announce here would ship a wrong record in product code: printing 'unimplemented; ignored' about a read that is implemented and is not ignored.*  **REACHABILITY: all six are reachable by an ordinary guest load on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`**, and the addresses are derived rather than guessed -- `machine_sgi.c:500` passes base `0x15000000`, `dev_sgi_re_init` registers `sgi_re` at `baseaddr + 0x1000` for `DEV_SGI_RE_LENGTH` (0x1000), and the handler restores the full offset with `relative_addr += 0x1000`.  **Every window lies inside `0x15001000-0x150017ff`, none is shadowed, and the `else if` cascade is strictly disjoint.**  Verified here independently by opening `machine_sgi.c` and `crmfbreg.h`: the six constants are `0x1000 / 0x1200 / 0x1400 / 0x1600 / 0x1700 / 0x1780`.  **GUEST KSEG1 ADDRESSES, so a probe author does not re-derive them:** TLB_A `0xb5001000`, TLB_B `0xb5001200`, TLB_C `0xb5001400`, TEX `0xb5001600`, LINEAR_A `0xb5001700`, LINEAR_B `0xb5001780` -- read kills at lines 245, 262, 279, 296, 346, 362 respectively.  **One malloc serves all four handlers** (`dev_sgi_re_init` allocates one `struct sgi_re_data` and hands it to `sgi_de`, `sgi_mte` and `sgi_de_status`), so whatever latch infrastructure `#497` lands is already reachable here.  *The audit and the brief that produced it are preserved at `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/audit_20260830/` -- the ledger first cited the `_scratchpad/` copy, which the durable-path rule excludes precisely because it evaporates.  This row now has everything a rung-3 witness needs except the witness itself, which is the point of asking a seat for the addresses rather than the answer.*  *** THIS ROW'S TITLE SAID '21 TERMINATING CALLS INSIDE DEVICE_ACCESS' AND THAT WAS THE SAME CONFLATION I MADE AND CORRECTED ELSEWHERE TODAY.  Corrected 2026-08-30. ***  **21 is the FILE total.  15 are inside a `DEVICE_ACCESS`, and 13 of those in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)`** -- the other 6 live in `draw_primitive` (1) and `do_mte_transfer` (5), neither of which is a `DEVICE_ACCESS` and neither of which a guest reaches by an ordinary access.  *** AND THE TITLE WAS NOT CARELESS -- IT FAITHFULLY REPORTED WHAT THE TOOL SAID, WHICH WAS WRONG. ***  See `exitcensus`: `exit_census.py`'s per-file totals were correct all along, but its BY-ENCLOSING-CONSTRUCT split attributed every call in a plain non-static function to the preceding `DEVICE_ACCESS`.  That bucket is a REACHABILITY claim used for ranking -- *on a guest access path BY CONSTRUCTION* -- so the defect over-stated how many host kills a guest can actually reach.  Fixed; tree-wide the bucket falls **97 -> 83**.  *** THE ACCEPT DESIGN, ADJUDICATED 2026-08-30 -- AND IT REFUTES THE AUDIT RECORDED DIRECTLY ABOVE IT ON ONE ARM OF SIX. ***  **agy ruled ACCEPT for all six read arms on the ground that BACKING STATE EXISTS in the struct.  That is not the ACCEPT test.**  The doctrine's fourth option-set member -- minted from the footbridge IRQEnable precedent -- is *the model already computes the CORRECT answer and merely discards it*.  Existence of state is necessary and nowhere near sufficient.  **FIVE ARMS ARE CLEAN ACCEPT; TEX IS NOT.**  **TLB A/B/C:** the store packs big-endian halfword pairs from raw bus bytes and a readback through the same index expression round-trips BYTE-EXACTLY on this machine -- with a premise that belongs in the comment rather than assumed: `machine_sgi.c` pins BIG ENDIAN, and while the STORE path is byte-order-agnostic the READ path is not.  **LINEAR A/B:** the store splits `idata` into two uint32 cells and the readback is its exact inverse through the `readmax64`/`writemax64` pair -- byte-order symmetric BY CONSTRUCTION, so it needs no endianness premise at all.  *** TEX FAILS THE TEST, AND THE EVIDENCE IS A GREP: `re_tex` HAS ZERO CONSUMERS ANYWHERE. ***  `tlb_a/b/c` feed `horrible_getputpixel`; `linear_a/b` feed it and `do_mte_transfer`; **`re_tex` feeds nothing.**  Its state is written through the `>> 3` index that `sgitexindex` records as a defect -- frames overlap by three, 81 of 112 cells unreachable -- so **the model does not hold the right answer for TEX; it holds a self-consistent WRONG one.**  ACCEPT is available only in the MIRRORED form (the read uses the same `>> 3` expression), which round-trips per frame while shipping hardware-wrong reads at overlapping offsets.  **That is a materially weaker claim than the other five arms', and the round record must not blur them.**  **WHAT ACCEPT OWES THAT DECLINE DOES NOT -- five items, and the second is an ORDERING DEPENDENCY on `#497`:**  (1) **round-trip rows with DISTINCT sentinels per window** -- distinctness catches a cross-wired read, while a same-window index error breaks round-trip because the store frame is position-dependent.  The JOINT translation (store and read shifted together) escapes round-trip BY CONSTRUCTION -- clause (f) territory -- so the contract names it outside, and names honestly that an out-of-decode anchor channel exists for four arms (the rendering and MTE paths consume them) and **does not exist for `re_tex` today**.  (2) **a DEVICE-SIGNATURE control**, because a faithful round-trip through a device is value-indistinguishable from RAM -- the witness ladder's own two-control lesson.  The clean discriminator: **post-`#497` a 4-byte read DECLINES and returns 0 while RAM would serve it.**  So `#497` is an ordering dependency for this round's WITNESS QUALITY, not merely for its latch infrastructure.  (3) **the dead tail's first execution belongs to `#497`, not here.**  A width-declined READ falls through the splice and runs `memory_writemax64` with `odata = 0`.  This round's new surface is narrower than it looks: **NONZERO `odata` through the BE packing**, not 'the tail runs'.  (4) **the claim must be SCOPED**: *returns what the store path recorded*, never *faithful hardware readback*.  TEX refutes the stronger phrase today, and the LINEAR arms store the PROM's own wrongly-sign-extended values verbatim -- returning them faithfully is right; modelling hardware readback is unproven.  **That is `b716a34`'s lesson pre-applied: do not put the strong phrase in a site comment.**  (5) **a PRE-WRITE read row** returning 0, which pins the memset-init as guest-visible ABI and is the minimal de-fatalisation survival row.  *** TEX POISONS THE WARRANT, NOT THE ROUND -- AND THE COUPLING IS ENFORCEABLE BY ONE ROW RATHER THAN BY A DEPENDENCY. ***  Ship TEX MIRRORED plus its round-trip row, and any ONE-SIDED future fix reds that row loudly: store `>> 1` against read `>> 3` writes cells 4-7 and reads 1-4, and the reverse likewise, while the JOINT fix keeps identity and the row stays green.  **So the round-trip row IS the coupling enforcement**, and `sgitexindex`'s fix becomes a declared two-site edit with a tripwire already in place.  Recorded in BOTH rows.  **WHAT IS NOT ACCEPTABLE:** unmirrored `>> 1` reads over the `>> 3` store -- round-trip broken immediately, silent wrong data, worse than a decline -- or ACCEPT recorded with the same warrant strength as the other five arms.  **A LATENT THIS ROUND INHERITS RATHER THAN CREATES:** the TLB arms mask the INITIAL `tlbi` but increment it UNMASKED in the loop, so `relative_addr & 0x1ff >= 0x1fa` with `len 8` runs to index 0x102 -- three halfwords into the adjacent array.  Guest-unreachable for the alignment reason `sgilinearoob` settles path-by-path, and **a mirrored read arm makes it a second SITE of that class** -- record it there so an eventual bound fixes both directions.  *** THE TEST THAT KILLED `sgideoob` WAS THEN APPLIED TO THE SURVIVORS, BY ENUMERATION RATHER THAN BY REASONING -- AND BOTH HOLD.  2026-08-30. ***  Withdrawing a false member is only half the follow-through; the other half is asking whether the same miss is in the rows that remain.  The refuting method for `sgideoob` was *what is this index's ACTUAL RANGE?* -- so every reachable `relative_addr` in each window was walked and the resulting index recorded.  **TLB LOOP** (`re_tlb_a[256]`, `tlbi = ((ra & 0x1ff) >> 1) & 0xff`, four writes at `len 8`): with 8-byte ALIGNMENT -- the only thing a guest can produce -- the maximum index written is **255, IN BOUNDS**.  Unaligned, reachable only by the debugger's `put d`, it is **258, OOB by three halfwords**.  *Exactly as recorded: guest-unreachable, debugger-reachable, same disposition as `sgilinearoob`.*  **`sgitexindex`** (`re_tex[112]`, `tlbi = ((ra & 0xff) >> 3) & 0xff`, window `0x1600-0x16df`): maximum index written is **30, comfortably IN BOUNDS of [112]** -- so this row is NOT an out-of-bounds and never claimed to be.  Its three stated figures were each re-derived independently and all match: **81 cells unreachable**, **3 entries of overlap** between consecutive 8-byte writes.  *** SO THE CLASS AFTER THE WITHDRAWAL IS: TWO REAL OOBs (`sgilinearoob`, the TLB loop), BOTH GUEST-SHIELDED BY ALIGNMENT AND BOTH DEBUGGER-REACHABLE; ONE NON-OOB CORRECTNESS DEFECT (`sgitexindex`); AND ONE WITHDRAWN FALSE MEMBER. ***  Every one now tested by the same method, which is what the false member's whole lesson was.  *** THE WITNESS FIELD WAS STALE AND SAID THE OPPOSITE OF WHAT SHIPPED.  Corrected 2026-08-30 by the pass-2 seat. ***  It read *no rung-3 probe has been built and the round owes one before any edit* -- **false since `#497` shipped six `ld kill` rows**, each a real guest `ld` at one of the six windows, dying with per-site attribution text, on an unmodified machine.  The row's note mentioned them nowhere.  *** AND THE SAME ROWS CHANGE THIS ROW'S COST IN THE OTHER DIRECTION, WHICH MUST BE WRITTEN DOWN NOW RATHER THAN REDISCOVERED: FIXING THIS DEFECT WILL TURN A GREEN GATE RED BY SUCCEEDING. ***  Six rows' worth.  That is precisely the `n6` shape adjudicated hours earlier in this same session, so this round inherits its ordering obligation: **re-pin the six rows FIRST, measure the committed build RED under the new expectations, and only then edit** -- same scripts, opposite colour.  Without that order the re-pin is observationally identical to fitting the test to the binary.  *** THE HEADLINE COUNT INCLUDES ONE SITE THAT CAN NEVER EXECUTE.  Derived 2026-08-30. ***  `dev_sgi_re.c` holds 20 raw `exit(1)` lines; exactly FIVE are commented out (179, 188, 280, 630, 1122) and fifteen are live -- **including 1334, which is inside `sgi_mte`'s unreachable `else` (see `sgimtewidth`).**  **`exit_census.py` excludes COMMENTED-OUT code and cannot see REACHABILITY.**  That is correct behaviour for a text-level tool and it is a limit worth stating rather than discovering: \"live terminating calls\" means SYNTACTICALLY live.  The row's numbers are not wrong -- they are the census's numbers -- but a round quoting them as reachable host kills would be over-claiming by one.  *** THE `sgirekills` / `sgitexindex` COUPLING, ESTABLISHED 2026-08-31 AND RECORDED ON BOTH ROWS SO NEITHER CAN BE CLOSED IN IGNORANCE OF THE OTHER. ***  **The fact, which is settled:** `sgitexindex`'s `>> 1` is correct under BOTH candidate readings of a TEX entry -- 112 halfwords indexed `offset >> 1`, or 28 64-bit entries where entry `k` starts at halfword `4k`, since `(offset >> 3) * 4 == offset >> 1` for 8-aligned offsets.  **So the store's shift is wrong INDEPENDENTLY of the missing datasheet**, and there is no SGI document in `_scratchpad/`.  **Consequence for `sgirekills`:** its TEX read arm can only ACCEPT in the MIRRORED form -- reusing the store's `>> 3` -- because only that round-trips what the store wrote.  Mirroring therefore enshrines a KNOWN-WRONG index in a second place, and gives it a green round-trip row.  *When someone later corrects the shift, that row goes RED because of their correct fix.*  **Consequence for `sgitexindex`, in the other direction:** the TEX read arm is TODAY a live host kill, so **no guest can read back what those writes stored** and the aliasing is currently unobservable from inside the guest.  Fixing `sgirekills` is what would expose it.  That bears on severity and on ORDER, and neither row had recorded it.  *Neither consequence decides the disposition -- that is the question `sgirekills` is held for and a panel is answering it.  What is recorded here is the COUPLING, which is a fact either way.*  *** THE HELD TEX-READ QUESTION IS ANSWERED: DO NOT MIRROR.  3 of 3 packet-fed seats, and the dissenter's FALLBACK is the other two's first choice, so the convergence is total. ***  deepseek and minimax: **decline + latched announce** until the index fix lands.  glm: **BACKED ACCEPT with the CORRECT `>> 1` index**, round-trip oracle pinned to correct semantics and honestly red until `sgitexindex` lands -- *with decline + latched announce as its explicit fallback, and mirrored ACCEPT ranked worst of the three by all three seats.*  **MY PREMISE WAS CIRCULAR AND A SEAT SAID SO USING MY OWN INLINED FINDING.**  See its entry above.  The practical upshot: the choice is between *decline* and *correct-index read*, and MIRRORING -- the option the row was held on -- is off the table.  *One caution against glm's first choice, which it half-concedes: a correct-index read against a wrong-index store means a guest that writes then reads gets data from a different place, which is arguably worse for the guest than a decline returning 0.  That is why all three seats land on decline as at least an acceptable answer.*  **AND ONE OBJECTION OF ITS OWN IS ANSWERED BY A GUARD I FAILED TO INLINE.**  glm marked the *31 of 112 / overlap by three* arithmetic NOT SETTLEABLE because it presupposes 8-byte-wide 8-byte-aligned stores, asserted nowhere in the packet.  Correct as a packet criticism: `#497`'s width guard on this very arm declines `len != 8`, so the width is established, and 8-byte ALIGNMENT follows from the MIPS path (`ld`/`sd` raise ADEL/ADES before `memory_rw`, and the unaligned helper masks the address itself).  **A packet defect of mine, not an analysis defect of its.**",
regress/artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:5678:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:25130:   "note": "*** THE WIDEST DOOR IN THIS DEVICE, AND NO LEDGER ROW COVERED IT.  Found by the adjudicating seat while ranking the OTHER four sgi_re rows; verified here by opening the file and deriving the gap arithmetic. ***  **THE SITE** is the final `else` of the window cascade in `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)` -- a `fatal()` for each direction and then a bare `exit(1)`, with **NO WIDTH PREDICATE AT ALL**.  Every other kill in this handler is reached only by choosing an unusual access WIDTH (the six `len != 8` arms) or a DIRECTION (the six read arms).  This one needs neither: any width, either direction, any unmatched address.  **THE DOOR, derived rather than asserted.**  `memory_device_register` covers `baseaddr + 0x1000` for `DEV_SGI_RE_LENGTH` = `0x1000`, and the handler restores the full offset with `relative_addr += 0x1000`, so the dispatched range is `0x1000-0x1fff`.  The six windows occupy `0x1000-0x17ff` with one internal gap:      TLB_A 0x1000-0x11ff   TLB_B 0x1200-0x13ff   TLB_C 0x1400-0x15ff      TEX   0x1600-0x16df   LINEAR_A 0x1700-0x177f  LINEAR_B 0x1780-0x17ff  leaving **`0x16e0-0x16ff` (32 bytes) plus `0x1800-0x1fff` (2048 bytes) = 2080 bytes** that fall through to the kill.  Guest KSEG1: a single `lb` at `0xb5001800` ends the host process.  *** IT SURVIVES BOTH ROUNDS THAT LOOK LIKE THEY WOULD FIX IT, WHICH IS WHY IT MUST BE FILED BEFORE THE NEXT COMPLETENESS SENTENCE SHIPS. ***  `#497` repairs the six WIDTH guards and never reaches this arm.  `sgirekills` as scoped repairs the six READ arms inside the windows and never reaches it either.  A reader of either round record could reasonably conclude the device no longer kills on a stray access.  It does.  **AND ITS FIX CANNOT BE ACCEPT**, which is what keeps it out of `sgirekills`' round rather than a scheduling preference: nothing backs an unmapped address, so there is no stored value to return.  It is DECLINE + a latched `(once)` announce -- a different fix design, so the round-scope rule's same-fix-design arm fails.  *The agy audit listed this arm as '1 unhandled offset fallback' and never asked whether it was reachable.  A census that enumerates a site without ranking it is how the widest door stays open.*  *** DESIGN SETTLED 2026-08-30 BY ADJUDICATION, BEFORE EITHER ROUND STARTS, SO NEITHER BURNS ITS PANEL ARGUING SCOPE. ***  **TWO ROUNDS, and both separate from `#497` -- all THREE round-scope conjuncts fail, worked through rather than asserted.**  SAME DEVICE fails: `sgi_de` and `sgi_re` are separate `memory_device_register` registrations at `baseaddr+0x2000` and `+0x1000`.  SAME SEMANTIC CLASS fails: `sgidewidth` is the WIDTH class with a real accept set `{4,8}` a decline must PRESERVE, while `sgireunmapped` is the BADOFFSET class where nothing legal reaches the arm at all.  **SAME DETECTOR / NO NEW ORACLE fails, and this is the one that decides it:** `sgi_de`'s registers are READABLE so its rows read back through `odata` -- a real device-signature channel -- while `sgi_re`'s else arm has NO BACKING STORE and its rows can only observe the diagnostic and the guest-gets-zero channel.  *Those are different oracles in kind, and the tempting collapse -- 'both are a refused access ending the host' -- is exactly what the no-new-oracle clause exists to refuse.*  *** THE CHANNEL FINDING, AND IT IS THE MOST IMPORTANT LINE IN THIS BLOCK. ***  `#497`'s `d_returnzero` escaped 35/35 and the reason generalises to BOTH these rounds: **the `(once)` announce prints IDENTICALLY for a correct decline and for a `return 0`**, because the announce happens before the return statement.  So the DIAGNOSTIC channel does not kill it either -- and neither does host survival, since a guest bus error leaves the host alive.  **The only discriminator is what the GUEST RECEIVED.**  Every round in this family owes a SENTINEL-SEEDED READ ROW: seed the destination register, perform the declined read, and assert it reads back 0 -- a correct decline delivers zero, `return 0` faults and the register keeps its seed.  *That is the row `#497` was missing, and the measuring seat reached the same repair independently while this adjudication was being written.*  **TWO LATCH BITS, NOT ONE, and the precedent is MEASURED rather than stylistic.**  The arm prints two distinct messages -- `unimplemented write to address` and `unimplemented read from address`.  `#490` built the one-bit form as `M3shared` and **it ESCAPED**: a session declining at both sites sees the second fall silent because the first already latched.  `#279` states the rule outright -- *one flag per DISTINCT MESSAGE and never one shared flag*.  A single bit here means the first stray READ silences the first stray WRITE's message forever, and vice versa.  **THE DE-ESCALATION ACCEPT CLAUSE IS N/A, WITH ITS REASON RECORDED -- not unrun.**  The clause enumerates predicates that used to gate death and pins each accept side.  This arm **has no predicate**: it is the fall-through when none of the six window conditions match, so there is no adjacent modelled value because **nothing legal reaches it**.  The enumeration is the empty set.  *Adding a row to test the WINDOW predicates instead would be the addition licence's 'cannot hurt' wearing detector clothes -- and it is unnecessary, because `#497`'s own accept rows already prove those addresses reach their own arms rather than this one.  That is inherited coverage, not an uncovered hole.*  **WHAT IT OWES INSTEAD IS A CONTRACT SENTENCE (clause (e)):** this fix makes every unmatched address a silent zero-decline, so a future window-boundary regression that drops a legal address here no longer announces -- and that class is pinned by `#497`'s window accept rows, not by this arm.  *** AND A CONTRACT LIMIT WORTH KNOWING BEFORE ROWS ARE WRITTEN: the WRITE arm's `return 0` mutant is NOT distinguishable by a value-only cold-debugger probe. ***  A declined write and a faulting write both leave no readback.  The READ arm is killable by the seeded-read row; the write arm needs exception-state observation, which is a heavier probe shape than `#497` used.  **State it as a limit rather than shipping a completeness claim the rows cannot support.**  *** THE 32-BYTE GAP IS NOT DEAD SPACE.  IT IS `CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS`, A SEVENTH REAL HARDWARE WINDOW. ***  `crmfbreg.h` defines `CRIME_RE_CLIP_IDS 0x16e0` between `CRIME_RE_TEX 0x1600` and `CRIME_RE_LINEAR_A 0x1700`, and `struct sgi_re_data` carries `// todo: clip_ids registers.` exactly where they would go.  The codex seat reached the same conclusion from the CRIME spec and put it best: **\"six real windows\" should read \"six MODELED windows\"** -- CLIP_IDS is real hardware with no dispatch and no state.  **The 2080-address count is UNCHANGED** (CLIP_IDS still falls to the kill) **but the characterisation splits, and the decline message must follow it:** 32 bytes are a documented, unmodelled register block; 2048 are genuinely unmapped.  A single \"unimplemented address\" diagnostic is accurate for the 2048 and WRONG for the 32.  *The brief asked whether `CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0` was a bug or the gap was real; both options were wrong, and a seat answering as posed would have had to pick the wrong one of two.  Three local facts agree the bound is deliberate: the next defined constant, the struct's own todo comment, and `re_tex[112]` x 2 bytes = 0xe0 exactly.*  *** THE fable5 SEAT'S EQUIVALENCE CLAIM IS REFUTED, AND THE REFUTATION CHANGES A DISPOSITION RATHER THAN SCORING A POINT. ***  It reported that an early `return 1` after the announce is OBSERVATIONALLY EQUIVALENT because *\"`memory_rw.c`'s #95 block memsets `data` to 0 on EVERY device read before the handler runs\"*, and advised disposing of that mutant as EQUIVALENT rather than chasing it.  **Checked both halves and both are false.**  The device handler is called at `memory_rw.c` `res = mem->devices[i].f(cpu, mem, paddr, data, len, ...)` and NOTHING zeroes `data` before it -- the three `memset(data, 0, len)` sites in that file are all on NON-DEVICE paths (the MIPS Sprite area, the beyond-physical_max arm, and the `memblock == NULL` unmapped-RAM arm), each followed by a `goto do_return_ok`.  And the MIPS caller's buffer is `uint8_t data[LS_SIZE];`, an **UNINITIALISED STACK ARRAY** with no memset anywhere in that file.  **SO THE MUTANT IS NOT EQUIVALENT -- BUT THE CORRECT DISPOSITION IS NOT 'DETECTABLE' EITHER, AND THAT NUANCE IS THE POINT.**  Skipping the tail leaves the guest whatever was on the stack, which is neither 0 nor any fixed value: the observable is REAL but NONDETERMINISTIC.  A row asserting a specific sentinel would be FLAKY, not sound.  *Filing it as EQUIVALENT would have retired a live mutant on a false mechanism; filing it as ordinary coverage debt would invite a flaky row.  It is neither, and the round must say so.*  **The seat's own remedy for the sibling `return 0` case is the one that works here too: seed the destination register with a sentinel and prove execution continued past the load** -- which pins the mutant without depending on stack contents.  *Everything else in that answer verified: `dev_unreadable.c` IS `return 0;` and `machine_sgi.c` installs it TEN times, so the FAULT member is real and in use on this machine.*  *** THE PASS-1 PANEL RESOLVED THE ROUND'S ONLY DISAGREEMENT BY WARRANT, NOT BY VOTE. ***  grok said ONE latch bit, deepseek said TWO.  I grepped first and found ZERO device files in the tree splitting a latch by direction -- which looked like precedent for grok.  **fable5 showed the framing was wrong: the house latch is per-MESSAGE-SITE, and this arm has TWO message texts (the write arm prints the data value, the read arm does not).  So two bits is not a new rule, it is the existing rule counted correctly** -- and my grep found nothing because I searched for the wrong property.  Two further warrants: a declined READ fabricates data the guest acts on while a declined write is a no-op, and one shared bit order-couples the two message rows so 'read message exactly once' becomes unpinnable.  *** WITNESSED AT RUNG 3, 32/32.  The row's witness field said `none`. ***  **AND ONE ROW FAILED FOR A REASON WORTH KEEPING: `lw` from 0x16ff SURVIVED** -- the log shows `exception ADEL`, because an unaligned load is refused by the CPU before `memory_rw` is reached.  Re-cut to 0x16fc it kills.  *The row was wrong, not the code, and the seat said so rather than adjusting the claim to fit.*  **THE ESCAPES, ranked by how little they cost:**  (1) *** A WINDOW-BOUND SHRINK (`+ 0x80` -> `+ 0x40`) SCORED A TOTAL ESCAPE, 9/9, AGAINST THE FIRST DETECTOR -- because the accept row wrote to the window's BASE, where a move of the FAR bound is invisible by construction. ***  That is clause (f)'s anchor rule arriving on the accept side, and the repair is one word: **an accept row must use the window's LAST slot.**  (2) a latch-bit collision reusing `SGIRE_W_TEX_LEN` scores 13/14, caught only by a row that touches another latch site first.  (3) `return 0` escapes both \"host survives\" and \"message once\" -- and the seat measured the discriminator side by side: with `v0` pre-set to **0** the row PASSES the mutant; pre-set to **0xa5a5a5a5** it FAILS, because a DBE leaves the register untouched.  The log is decisive (`reading from device 'sgi_re' ... failed` / `exception DBE`).  **So the brief's stated pin \"the declined read returns 0\" is MEASURED VACUOUS unless the sentinel is non-zero.**  **A REFINEMENT TO THE SIX-MEMBER OPTION SET, and it is not a quibble:** the set collapses THREE DISTINCT ANNOUNCE DISCIPLINES living in this one file -- unbounded-and-always-visible (`fatal()`, in sgi_de and sgi_mte), **unbounded but VERBOSITY-GATED (`debug()`, in sgi_de_status, invisible at default verbosity)**, and latched-once.  Folding the second into \"already declines\" hides exactly the flood question the round has to answer.  *** ROW COVERAGE, STATED RATHER THAN GLOSSED: ONLY 10 OF 14 ROWS EVER FIRED, AND THREE OF THE FOUR SILENT ONES ARE AN UNDISCHARGED OBLIGATION. ***  `accept tlba`, `accept tlbb` and `accept tlbc` never fired because **no shrink mutant was built for the three TLB windows**.  By the accept-row failability rule an accept row is discharged by a MEASURED KILL, not by existing -- so these three are NOT discharged, and the round owes three more mutants (about 1.5 s per build).  **AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME MUTANT SHAPE AS THE FOUR ALREADY BUILT.**  TLB_A and TLB_B are bounded by *the next window's constant* while the other four use *base + size*, so shrinking a TLB bound perturbs TWO arms at once -- it moves a boundary two windows share, rather than opening a gap.  A mutant written on the base+size pattern would not exercise them.  The fourth silent row, `read0 VACUOUS`, is **deliberately** never red: it is the control that DEMONSTRATES the brief's stated pin is vacuous.  Same load, `v0` pre-set to 0 instead of 0xa5a5a5a5, and it PASSES on the mutant where the real row fails.  *A row that never fires because it is a negative control and a row that never fires because nobody built its mutant must not be counted alike, which is why they are separated here.*  **DISPOSITION: this is L3, an undischarged obligation, not coverage debt** -- the three rows are the sole carriers of the de-escalation accept side for their windows, and the round's claim to have shipped accept rows is false about them until the mutants exist.  It is owed BEFORE the fix ships, not filed after.  *** RETRACTED.  MY REFUTATION OF fable5's EQUIVALENCE CLAIM WAS WRONG, AND I READ THE PRISTINE 2021 UPSTREAM COPY OF `memory_rw.c` TO PRODUCE IT.  fable5 WAS RIGHT. ***  I wrote that *\"nothing zeroes `data` before the handler call -- the three `memset(data, 0, len)` sites are all on NON-DEVICE paths\"*, and shipped it in `a115a4a`.  **The fork's `src/cpus/memory_rw.c` has SIX such sites, and one of them is exactly what fable5 described**, inside the device-found branch, before `paddr -= baseaddr` and before the dispatch:  `/*  #95: zero the caller's buffer up front on a read so a device handler that fills only part of it ... can never hand back uninitialised host memory to the guest.  */  if (writeflag == MEM_READ) memset(data, 0, len);`  *** HOW IT HAPPENED, AND IT IS THE HAZARD THIS PROJECT DOCUMENTS IN CAPITALS FOR SEATS -- IT CAUGHT THE MAIN LOOP INSTEAD. ***  There are two `memory_rw.c` under the project root: the fork at **794 lines** (`GXEMUL-SEC`, `est`, `build`, `build-asan`, md5 3d47dd66) and a PRISTINE 2021 UPSTREAM copy at **530 lines** (`./src/cpus/`, md5 75fbe149, plus two archive copies).  My grep ran as `src/cpus/memory_rw.c` while the cwd had drifted to the PROJECT ROOT, so it resolved to the upstream file.  **Every line number I reported -- 161, 222, 425, 437, 486 -- is below 530, and the fork's are 215/267/492/671/683/732.  The numbers themselves were the tell and I did not read them as one.**  **THE CONSEQUENTIAL ERROR, not the embarrassing one:** I dispositioned the early-`return 1` mutant as *\"REAL but NONDETERMINISTIC -- a row asserting a sentinel would be FLAKY\"*.  With #95 in place the guest gets a DETERMINISTIC ZERO, so on the value channel that mutant is **EQUIVALENT**, and a sentinel row is not flaky -- it is simply BLIND to it.  The opus5 seat measured this with two builds differing only in #95's fill byte: filling 0x00 gives `lbu = 0x0`, filling 0xa5 gives `lbu = 0xa5`, tracking byte-for-byte.  *So the disposition inverts: not coverage debt on a flaky channel, but an EQUIVALENT mutant that the mutant-that-will-not-die rule says to dispose of as a RESULT.  In `sgi_de` it is not equivalent overall, because that tail also DRAWS and `dec_switch` separates them -- but in `sgi_re`, whose tail is bare, opus5 expects true equivalence.*  **AND I TOLD THE OWNER I HAD REFUTED A SEAT.**  The correction is recorded here, in the carrier, and reported back -- because a wrong refutation that stands is worse than the original error: it teaches the next round to distrust the seat that was right.  *** L4 -- THE REASON THIS ROW GIVES FOR ITS OWN GAP IS WRONG FOR ONE OF ITS THREE MEMBERS, found 2026-08-31 by #499's PRE-TABLE block (sibling parity) before any mutant was built. ***  The note explains all three missing kills with *the TLB windows are bounded by the NEXT window's constant, not base+size*.  Re-derived from the file: that is TRUE of TLB_A (`< CRIME_RE_TLB_B`) and TLB_B (`< CRIME_RE_TLB_C`) and **FALSE of TLB_C, which is `< CRIME_RE_TLB_C + 0x200` -- structurally IDENTICAL to TEX, LIN_A and LIN_B, all three of which got a shrink mutant and all three of which FIRED.**  So `accept tlbc` was never blocked by window shape; it was simply not built.  **The asymmetry is TWO arms, not three, and a third of the filed obligation is the same edit that already worked three times.**  Recorded because a wrong reason gets reused: a future round reading this note would have gone looking for a special mutant shape for a window that needs the ordinary one.  Durable anchor: `grep -nE 'relative_addr < CRIME_RE' src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c` prints all six far bounds in one screen, which is what made the asymmetry visible at all.  *** #499's MUTANT TABLE (regress/artifacts/sgireunmapped/killtable_20260831/): THE THREE ACCEPT ROWS ARE DISCHARGED, AND THE SEAT OVERTURNED MY FRAMING TWICE. ***  **MY PREMISE WAS FALSE AND I GAVE IT TO THE SEAT AS SETTLED.**  I briefed that the `exit(1)` \"was replaced by a latched refusal\", reading the row's `commits: a643e18` as the fix.  **At HEAD the catch-all is STILL `exit(1)`** -- `grep -n 'unimplemented read from address' src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c`, with the kill two lines below.  `a643e18` shipped the WITNESS and the analysis; **#499 is the round that ships the fix**, not one auditing a shipped one.  *A premise-audit opener would have caught this and my brief did not carry one -- I wrote the settled-premises block and then stated a premise in it that was wrong.*  The seat also corrected my expected-results line: I asked for a baseline that is all-green, and **base is 10/14 BY DESIGN** -- that is test-first, and the all-green control is the FIX.  **THE TABLE.  base 10/14, fix 14/14, and each shrink reddens EXACTLY its own row:** `M1 tlba_shrink` 13/14 (`accept tlba`), `M2 tlbb_shrink` 13/14 (`accept tlbb`), `M3 tlbc_shrink` 13/14 (`accept tlbc`).  **No shrink perturbs a neighbour**, which also refutes this row's own \"a shrink to a next-constant bound moves a boundary two windows share\" -- editing the `<` EXPRESSION opens a gap and does not touch the sibling arm; only editing `CRIME_RE_TLB_B` in `crmfbreg.h` would.  **So all three accept rows now carry measured kills and the L3 is dischargeable.**  **M4 bitcollide ESCAPED at 14/14, exactly as the PRE-TABLE block predicted** -- and the prediction was measured rather than trusted.  The seat then BUILT the killing row: **one emulator, two NARROW (`len != 8`) accesses, one in TLB_A and one in TLB_B**, oracle `count(\"CRIME_RE_TLB_B -- declined\") == 1`.  The session shape is the whole point -- the accept rows do ONE 8-byte store in a fresh emu, so they never enter a width guard and never set a latch bit, making the collision unobservable to them BY CONSTRUCTION.  Generalised for one session's cost: six narrow accesses, one per window, asserting all six decline texts appear, which pins every pairwise collision among the six bits.  *** AND A DEFECT NEITHER OF US HAD ENUMERATED: `#498` SILENTLY BROKE THIS ROW'S WITNESS GENERATOR. ***  `opus_witness_20260830/mkvariant.py` inserts `SGIRE_W_UNMAP_WR = 1 << 6`; `#498`/`cb28a6f` added `SGIRE_W_DE_LEN = 1 << 6` to the same enum.  **A duplicate enumerator is legal C and silent**, and `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_de)` casts the same `struct sgi_re_data *`, so `d->warned` is shared across both devices.  Measured as `fix6`: **14/14, total escape**, with a transcript showing one guest `sb` to sgi_de permanently silencing sgi_re's catch-all diagnostic.  The replacement `mkvariant499.py` uses `1<<7`/`1<<8` and hard-fails on duplicates.  *A cross-round interaction: one round's enum addition invalidated another round's generator, with no gate able to see it.*  *** M5 -- THE ACCEPT SUITE TESTS THE FAR BOUND AND NEVER THE NEAR BOUND, AND THIS FALSIFIES A CARRIER RULE MINTED FROM THIS VERY ROW EARLIER THE SAME DAY.  Ranked a FLOOR BREACH (clause (d), L2) by the measuring seat. ***  The carrier said: *an accept row written at a window's BASE tests the near bound twice and the far bound never -- put it at the LAST legal slot, **where both bounds are load-bearing***.  The first half is right and is why these rows were moved to the top slot. **The second half is measured FALSE.**  `M5`: `>= CRIME_RE_LINEAR_A` -> `>= CRIME_RE_LINEAR_A + 0x40`, **one token**, scores a **14/14 CLEAN SWEEP** -- while a control row storing at the window BASE reds on M5 and is serviced normally on the fix.  **Moving the rows from base to top SWAPPED the blindness rather than removing it.**  **The reason is structural, not a detail of this device:** an accept row's oracle is *this access was serviced*, which is a single bit, and ONE bound can explain that bit on its own.  So there is no slot from which both bounds are observable, and a single accept row pins exactly the bound it is near.  **THE REPAIR COSTS NO EXTRA SESSIONS AND IS STRICTLY BETTER THAN TWO ROWS:** the store's own `debug()` already prints the destination -- `[ d->re_linear_b[30] = 0x... ]`.  Assert **the array NAME and the INDEX** rather than merely that the catch-all was missed.  That converts each relative row into an ANCHORED row in clause (f)'s sense, pins BOTH bounds from one access, and additionally catches TRANSLATIONS -- which no base-or-top accept row can see at all.  Two rows (base AND top) is the fallback where no such channel exists, and it is weaker: it pins two bounds and still cannot see a re-map that keeps both.  **Because M5 weakens a predicate the fix DEPENDS ON and dominates the edited catch-all, it is clause (d) and therefore L2 -- repaired IN-ROUND, not filed as coverage debt.**  The carrier has been corrected in place; the detector repair ships with the fix.  *This is the freshly-minted-exemplar rule firing on itself: a rule minted from ONE instance carried that instance's shape as if it were the class, and the first round to APPLY it measured the general claim false within hours.  The instruction survived; the universal clause did not.*  *** LATCH-BIT DECONFLICTION BETWEEN TWO ROUNDS IN FLIGHT, caught 2026-08-31 BEFORE either shipped rather than measured afterwards. ***  `dev_sgi_re.c`'s `SGIRE_W_*` enum uses bits **0-6** today (`TLBA/TLBB/TLBC/TEX/LINA/LINB` 0-5, `DE_LEN` 6).  **`#499` takes `1 << 7` (`SGIRE_W_UNMAP_WR`) and `1 << 8` (`SGIRE_W_UNMAP_RD`)**, per its measured `mkvariant499.py` fix.  **`#500` was designed for `SGIRE_W_PRIMOP = 1 << 7` and MUST TAKE `1 << 9` instead.**  *A duplicate enumerator is legal C and silent, and both devices cast the same `struct sgi_re_data *`, so a collision makes one arm's decline permanently suppressible by unrelated traffic to the other -- measured as a 14/14 total escape when it happened between `#498` and `#499`'s generator.*  *** THE PORT SHIPPED AND IT CAUGHT THREE MUTANTS THE ARTIFACT DETECTOR LET THROUGH -- which is the L2 repair working, measured on the artifact that will actually run rather than inherited from the one that will not.  `regress/sgi_re_unmapped_probe.py`, 28 rows, self-contained, artifacts under killtable_20260831/port/. ***  **Re-measured on the SHIPPED probe:** `m4_bitcollide` **14/14 ESCAPED -> 27/28 KILLED**; `m5_lina_near` **14/14 -> 25/28** (three rows); the duplicate-1<<6 variant **14/14 -> 27/28**.  Base 10/14 -> **18/28**, fix 14/14 -> **28/28**.  Five further mutants built and killed: `mret0` 26/28, `m6_idxshift` 26/28, `m7_wintrans` 24/28, `mnolatch` 26/28, `mbothsame` 27/28, `mrdcollide` 27/28.  *** THE TRANSLATION CLAIM WAS MEASURED AND CAME BACK NARROWER THAN I STATED IT, which is why it was asked for as a measurement. ***  I recorded that the anchored-oracle repair *should also catch translations*.  Measured: **anchoring ALONE does not catch a translation; anchoring PLUS a base-slot row does.**  Under `m7_wintrans` the TOP-slot row `tlba acc hi` stayed **GREEN** -- a row inside the moved window cannot see that the window moved -- while `tlba acc lo` reddened by falling out, and `tlbb acc lo` reddened on the MIS-ATTRIBUTION channel (the transcript says `d->re_tlb_a[0]` where the row wants `d->re_tlb_b[0]`), which no unanchored oracle can see at all.  An index re-map (`m6_idxshift`) IS caught outright, both rows.  **So the true claim is: anchored + BOTH bounds catches index re-maps outright and catches window translations at the base row.**  *** AND THE SEAT CHECKED WHAT ALREADY EXISTED BEFORE BUILDING WHAT I ASKED FOR. ***  I proposed generalising the latch-collision row to all six windows.  `sgi_re_width_probe.py`'s `xsite 6msg` **already ships that exact generalisation, is gate-wired, and measured 32/35 against m4** -- so a duplicate would have been a row that can never fail alone.  It re-aimed the idea at THIS round's own two bits instead (`xwin wr`/`xwin rd` walk all six #497 latches plus #498's sgi_de bit, since the four handlers share one malloc'd struct), with `xwin7 pre` as the partner control.  **ONE ROW DELIBERATELY DROPPED and one row's base value deliberately CHANGED, both disclosed rather than buried:** `read0 VACUOUS` was a pedagogical control that cannot fail under the mutant it names, so it is gone and its lesson survives as the reason `rd sent` presets a sentinel; and `latch fresh` (green on base, because base ALSO prints its text before dying) became `xwin wr`, which counts the `-- declined.` literal and is RED on base.  *A port that silently changes a score is the failure mode here, so both are named.*  **COVERAGE DEBT, stated rather than glossed: 8 of 28 rows never failed on any of 14 variants.**  Two are the witness ladder's clause-(ii) controls (`live`, `devsig`) and fire only if the harness or machine breaks.  The other six are per-window accept replicas of shapes discharged elsewhere, filed WITH the mutants that would discharge them (near-grow at TLB_C, far-shrink at TEX/LIN_B, near-grow at LIN_B).  **The seat stopped rather than chase per-row, which is the `#392` shape** -- the right call.  *One caveat it raised against its own work: `xwin7 pre` is a control that a product defect can redden (m4, m5, m7).  That is a valid kill, but the round must not cite it as the PRIMARY m4 detector -- `xsite 6msg` names that properly.*  *** INDEPENDENT REVIEW OF THE RE-PIN, 2026-08-31: DO NOT SHIP IT AS WRITTEN.  It repairs `otherkilldeath` and walks STRAIGHT INTO `exit0oracle`. ***  The proposed oracle is `exit0 + text`.  **Mutant: keep the fix's latch and message and add `exit(0);` after the if/else at the catch-all.**  The message prints (text True), the process exits 0 (`how == \"exit0\"`), `got == want` -> **GREEN, total escape** -- and `other kill` is the ONLY row in that 35-row probe that touches `OUTSIDE` (verified: two hits in the file, the definition and one use), so nothing else catches it.  *** AND THE KNOWLEDGE WAS ALREADY IN THE GATE, ADDRESSED TO THIS EXACT CLASS, WRITTEN THE SAME DAY. ***  `regress/gate_arm.sh` carries the flagship seat's note against re-pinning `tail_acc_x` onto `exit0` -- *exit status cannot vary between 'declined and then quit' and 'died with status 0'* -- together with the repair.  **The `#499` re-pin was written without it.**  That is this project's unread-review shape: the answer sat in a file the round had itself just edited.  **REPAIR, using material already in the probe:** assert a DEVICE SIGNATURE through the existing `DE_STATUS`/`DE_STAT_VAL` constants (the `devsig stat` idiom), giving survival AND identity in one channel; or the `lw surv` sentinel-preset idiom, which distinguishes *the tail ran and wrote odata 0* from *the load never completed*.  **Minimum:** `session()` already returns `alive` and the proposal throws it away.  **So the round owes a FOURTH mutant of its own -- `exit(0)` at the sgi_re catch-all -- and the order measurement must be RE-RUN with whichever oracle actually ships.**  Worth knowing: the NEW 28-row probe would survive that mutant; the escape is confined to the re-pinned shipped row, so this is a floor question about the RE-PIN, not about the new detector.  *** THE ORDER CLAIM OVERCLAIMS BY ONE WORD, AND A STRONGER WARRANT WAS ALREADY IN HAND. ***  `REPIN_REQUIRED.md` is headed *\"ORDER DISCIPLINE -- measured, not asserted\"*.  **The RED is measured; the ORDER is not** -- every artifact carries the same copy-time mtime, so nothing shows the oracle was written before the fix was built.  **But the oracle was PRE-REGISTERED in a committed artifact by a prior round**: `git log -S \"death is the shared answer\"` gives `112867a` (#497, 2026-08-30 22:41), the commit that filed `otherkilldeath` and prescribed this repair -- **six hours before** this round's baseline `3ded1e8` (2026-08-31 04:34).  *That is checkable by `git show` rather than by trusting a timestamp, and it is what should carry the not-laundering claim.*  L4: correct the heading.  *** THE PORTED DETECTOR IS INDEPENDENTLY VALIDATED BY THE COORDINATOR, NOT ONLY BY THE SEAT THAT WROTE IT.  2026-08-31, log preserved at `regress/artifacts/sgireunmapped/killtable_20260831/port/coordinator_validate.out`. ***  Run against the COMMITTED tree at `fe5e2c1` with `build/gxemul`, from the gate's own working directory: **`PROBE_RC=0`, `OPUS499T_CONTROL=OK`, `OPUS499T_RESULT=18/28`, `OPUS499T_DONE` present.**  18 ok + 10 FAIL = 28, so the tally is internally consistent and no row was lost.  **AND THE TEN RED ROWS ARE EXACTLY THE ONES THE FIX ADDRESSES**, which is the test-first evidence rather than a bare number:  `wr surv`, `rd surv` (the host dies today), `wr kill`, `rd kill` (the kill text is present today), `wr decl`, `rd decl` (the decline text is absent today), `rd sent` (the sentinel survives an unanswered read), `wr rd 2msg` (no per-direction messages exist yet), and `xwin wr`/`xwin rd` (the new latch bits do not exist yet).  **Every accept row and both witness-ladder controls stay GREEN**, so the detector is not simply red about everything.  *Three things this checks that a seat's summary cannot: that the probe RUNS from the gate's cwd rather than only the seat's, that it emits its completion sentinel so a score is a measurement rather than a truncation, and that the reddening rows are the RIGHT ones.  The figure matches the seat's report exactly, which is what makes it a confirmation rather than a second opinion.*  *** CLOSED 2026-08-31 at `9f666a8`. ***  The catch-all is a per-direction latched decline falling through to the common tail; live `exit(1)` in `dev_sgi_re.c` 14 -> 13.  Two bits (`1 << 7`, `1 << 8`) placed AFTER `SGIRE_W_DE_LEN` in numeric order, because an earlier draft left the enum ending at `1 << 6` and a maintainer scanning to the last enumerator would have taken an already-used bit.  **The re-pin that shipped is NOT the one proposed**: `exit0+txt` was measured to ESCAPE an `exit(0)` mutant, and the measuring seat's own first-choice replacement escaped two more, so the sentinel-preset read of the unmapped address itself is what shipped.  Detector wired in the same commit, both hygiene pins raised in the same commit, all four build trees propagated and both clearance parts verified.  *** L4 CORRECTION, FOUND BY THE LATE PASS 2 -- AND IT IS THE EVIDENCE THAT THE LATE PASS WAS NOT THEATRE. ***  The measuring seat wrote that the shipped oracle's three failures are *three DISTINCT values*, and I repeated it into the pass-2 brief and the round's report.  **It is two, not three.**  Measured, from `repin2/table500b.out`:  `mexit0` -> `exit0/txt/died`;  `mret0` -> `exit0/txt/0xdeadbeef`;  `mrdexit0` -> `exit0/txt/died`.  **`died` appears twice**, so it is three FAILURES carrying two DISTINCT values.  **The substance survives and the overclaim does not.**  What the measurement supports is that the sentinel channel VARIES where the alternatives do not -- N2 fails all three while N1 (the device-signature read) fails only one -- which is the argument that actually mattered.  What it does not support is the stronger *three distinct values* framing used to dismiss the one-channel-wearing-three-hats objection.  *Caught by a packet-fed seat reading the parenthetical `(died / 0xdeadbeef / died)` against the claim it was offered to support -- i.e. by checking a claim against its OWN cited evidence, which is the cheapest audit there is and which neither I nor the seat that wrote it performed.*  **AND IT IS DIRECT EVIDENCE ON THE QUESTION OF WHETHER A LATE PASS 2 IS WORTH RUNNING**: this round shipped without one, the gate refused the next commit for it, and the pass run afterwards found a wrong record in the round's own justification within minutes.  *** codex's SEMANTIC CORRECTION, recorded because the records use the wrong word: THIS IS NOT A DECLINE AT THE CALLBACK BOUNDARY. ***  The handler returns **1** for an unmatched access exactly as for a serviced one, so `memory_rw` sees a handled access either way.  What the fix changes is that the host survives and a latched message is printed -- the access is still ACCEPTED at the boundary and answered with a defined 0 on the read side.  **The six-member option set distinguishes DECLINE from ACCEPT by precisely this boundary**, so calling it a decline blurs the distinction the set exists to draw.  *The behaviour is right and the word is wrong; a future round reading `sgireunmapped` as a DECLINE precedent would be citing something that returns success.*  *** L2 FLOOR BREACH, MEASURED BY THE PASS-2 SEAT: TWO MUTANTS SWEEP ALL 28 ROWS CLEAN, AND ONE RESTORES THIS ROUND'S OWN CITED WITNESS VERBATIM.  Repair is owed IN-ROUND however late. ***  **`W` -- re-add the pre-`#499` `len != 8` kill at the top of the catch-all, lifted verbatim from `9f666a8^`.  28/28, zero red -- and `sb` AND `lb` at `0xb5001800` die, printing the pre-fix kill text.**  That is floor clause (b): a verbatim reinstatement of the removed defect passes the detector.  **`T` -- `relative_addr < CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0` -> `+ 0x100`, ONE HEX DIGIT.  28/28, zero red -- and `ld` at `0xb50016e0` dies with `TODO: read from CRIME_RE_TEX`**, handing 32 bytes back to `exit(1)`.  Clause (d): a bound relaxed on a predicate dominating the edited lines.  *** THE TWO REASONS ARE STRUCTURAL, NOT LUCKY, AND I VERIFIED BOTH. ***  **(1) EVERY catch-all row uses len 8 and nothing else.**  `store()` defaults to `OP_SD_T0_A3` and `load()` to `OP_LD_V0_AT`; `OP_SB_T0_A3` exists but is aimed at the six mapped windows.  **So the detector pins the DIRECTION axis -- which the contract argues at length -- and never the WIDTH axis at the catch-all, while the round's HEADLINE is *no width predicate at all* and its WITNESS is a `lb`.**  **(2) All four catch-all offsets (`0x800/0x8c0/0x900/0x9c0`) land in the SECOND component.**  The round's own CHANGELOG names two: `0x16e0-0x16ff` (32 bytes) and `0x1800-0x1fff` (2048).  **The 32-byte component has ZERO rows.**  *This is `#498`'s `len == 2` hole one round later, in the same file: a detector that exercises one axis exhaustively and the other not at all.*  **AND THE PRETABLE MANDATE WAS ONLY PARTLY DISCHARGED, which is why the CHANNEL check did not catch it.**  The block's own header names baseline `e898c84`, the ARTIFACT detector, and the subject *discharge accept tlba/tlbb/tlbc*.  It does not cover the port to 28 rows, the fix, the re-pin, or the two new bits -- its GUARD-FIELD section enumerates SEVEN bits and says *all seven distinct today*, written before `1<<7` and `1<<8` existed.  **The CHANNEL check was never run over the catch-all rows, which is precisely where both mutants escape.**  The block's own opening line says `#498` skipped it and paid; the same shape recurred here one scope level up.  *** THE L2 IS REPAIRED IN-ROUND AND MEASURED.  Detector 28 -> 34 rows; both escaping mutants now die.  Artifacts: killtable_20260831/l2repair/. ***    fix **34/34**  |  `T` (`CRIME_RE_TEX + 0xe0` -> `+ 0x100`) **33/34**, `clip decl` red, was 28/28  |  `W` (the pre-`#499` `len != 8` kill re-added verbatim) **30/34**, all four narrow rows red, was 28/28.  **Six rows: four on the WIDTH axis** (a byte write and a 4-byte read at the catch-all, each with a survival row AND a positive channel -- the decline announced, the sentinel replaced by a defined 0, because a survival-only row is satisfied by a mutant surviving for the wrong reason) **and two on the FIRST COMPONENT** at `RE + 0x6e0`, which the handler's own `+0x1000` makes `0x16e0`.  *** AND THE VERIFICATION HIT TWO TRAPS THIS PROJECT HAD ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN, BOTH MINE. ***  **(1)** The first `W` build died with `error: missing terminating \" character` -- the `\\n` in the inserted C string cooked into a REAL NEWLINE through the Bash -> heredoc -> Python path, splitting the literal.  **The measuring seat had warned me about this exact path in the same report** (*do not hand-type it ... it cost me one round-trip*) and the carrier prescribes `chr(92)`.  I hand-typed it.  Rebuilt with `chr(92)` plus a check that the literal landed on ONE line.  *The stale-binary guard is what caught it: the build failed, left the old binary, and the run ABORTED rather than scoring a false escape.*  **(2)** My extra confirmation -- *does `W` really restore the witness?* -- used a debugger `put b` and reported ZERO kill messages.  **`put b` never reaches the device**: the byte path passes `NO_EXCEPTIONS` and `memory_rw` skips the device call for `DM_DEFAULT` devices.  That is the trap measured on `pcc2` and recorded by me hours earlier.  **The probe's own rows use real guest `sb`/`lw` instructions, which is exactly why they caught `W` while the debugger poke saw nothing** -- so the four red rows are the evidence and the `put b` line is an apparatus artifact.  *** AND THE L2 REPAIR ITSELF HAD THE SAME DEFECT ONE LEVEL DOWN, FOUND BY THE PASS THAT THE `no recursion` RULING WOULD HAVE SKIPPED.  Detector 34 -> 36. ***  I diagnosed the breach as *one axis pinned exhaustively, the other not at all*, repaired the width axis at the addresses the mutants used, and left the identical hole at the other component: the two `clip` rows took `store()`'s default `OP_SD`, so the matrix read **three cells of a two-by-two** -- component1 x narrow had nothing.  **A guard re-added at the catch-all was caught; a guard re-added and CONDITIONED on the first component would not have been.**  Two rows added (`clip narrow surv`, `clip narrow decl`).  Measured:  fix **36/36**;  `T` **34/36** with TWO reds where the 34-row version had one;  `W` **30/36** with **SIX** reds where it had four -- narrow now catches at BOTH components.  *** THIS ALSO ANSWERS THE SEAT THAT DOUBTED THE ROWS: deepseek called the first-component rows *suspect as survival-only*, i.e. possibly unable to vary.  They vary -- both `decl` rows moved 1 -> 0 under `T` while their paired `surv` rows held.  The positive channel is doing the work, which is why each row was paired that way. ***  *Found by three packet-fed seats reviewing the repair -- while BLINDED, because my brief claimed to inline the diff and inlined zero hunks.  Two of them said so plainly rather than answering around it, and found the gap anyway.*",
regress/artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:6211:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:27997:   "note": "*** THE #443 BADVAL CLASS AT SCALE -- THE SAME SHAPE AS `gdromcondlies`, WHICH WAS RATED HIGH AND HAS SINCE BEEN FIXED, AND AS `sgideprimop`, FILED HIGH TONIGHT. ***  **Four registers accept exactly ONE 64-bit value and end the host process on every other**, and two more accept only a single-bit mask.  The author documented the decision rather than hiding it: each `fatal()` reads *\"from the only implemented value\"*.  That is an honest TODO expressed as a host kill.  **A guest here is firmware** -- SRM or NetBSD bringing up the chipset -- so the reaching value is whatever that firmware writes, not an attacker's choice.  A model that implements one value and dies on the rest converts every unmodelled firmware revision into a host death.  *** SCOPE, STATED SO IT IS NOT OVER-CLAIMED: the two RANGE guards (`dev >= 21`, `ofs > len`) are a DIFFERENT question from the four VALUE guards and are not analysed here. ***  A bounds guard that kills may be protecting a real array, in which case the disposition is decline-not-delete, and deciding that needs the array geometry -- which is exactly the check that withdrew `sgideoob` as a false OOB.  Named, not judged.  **SINGLE CONSUMER: `machine_alpha.c:61`, `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`.**  There is no alpha rig in this tree, which is why the file has escaped attention -- the same reason `dev_pcc2.c` did.  *No rig means no coverage rather than no bug.*  **COMPANION TO `pcc2widthkills`, AND DELIBERATELY EXAMINED SEPARATELY.**  Both files surfaced from one question -- is `dev_sgi_re.c` uniquely bad or uniquely looked at? -- but they are different classes: pcc2 is WIDTH kills (12 sites, all `len != 1`), this is VALUE kills.  Filing them as one row would have merged two fix designs, and the reopening rule already says a round takes one site unless the SAME fix design covers both.  *A census that finds two files at once is exactly where over-claiming starts.*  *** THIS ROW HAS NO RUNG-3 ATTEMPT RECORDED AT ALL, AND THERE IS A PLAUSIBLE TARGET IT NEVER NAMES.  Found 2026-08-31 by grepping the row for the attempt the 2026-08-27 lifecycle ruling requires. ***  The row reasons from *\"No rig means no coverage rather than no bug\"*, which is true about COVERAGE and is not the same claim as *no machine instantiates this device*.  **`src/machines/machine_alpha.c` calls `device_add(machine, \"lca\")` under `case ST_ALPHABOOK1:`, and that subtype is registered with the invocable alias `\"alphabook1\"`** (anchors: `grep -n 'device_add(machine, \"lca\")' src/machines/machine_alpha.c` and `grep -n 'alphabook1' src/machines/machine_alpha.c`).  So the prerequisite a rung-3 probe needs -- a real, committed, UNMODIFIED in-tree machine description that maps the device -- **exists**, and the invocation is `-E alpha -e alphabook1`.  *What is NOT established, and must not be assumed: whether that machine constructs far enough to accept a cold-debugger probe, and whether the four registers are reachable through its decode.  Those are exactly what the ATTEMPT is for.*  **The ruling's point is that the ask costs one invocation and nobody had made it** -- `witness: none` must mean *tried and could not*, never *did not think to*, and those two states must not render the same.  A seat that reports it cannot build one has given a real answer and the row proceeds as documented-only, with the attempt recorded.  *Filed as the next action on this row rather than done here: firing a probe contends with a measuring seat that was running when this was found, and a probe measured under load is the load-sensitive-oracle failure this project has already paid for once.*  *** THE STATED BLOCKER -- \"there is no rig\" -- IS NOT A REASON A RUNG-3 WITNESS IS IMPOSSIBLE, AND THIS PROJECT'S OWN MOST RECENT WITNESS REFUTES IT.  Measured 2026-08-31. ***  `#498`'s rung-3 witness for `sgidewidth` execs `gxemul -V -x -E sgi -e ip32 -M 64 0x80001000:<DUMMY>` and **`DUMMY` falls back to `/dev/null`** when `regress/dummy_kernel.bin` is absent (anchor: `grep -n 'DUMMY = ' regress/sgi_de_width_probe.py`).  **No disk image, no kernel, no boot -- a cold-debugger probe needs a machine that CONSTRUCTS and an empty load file, and that is all.**  Verified by running all four candidates under WSL with `<addr>:/dev/null`: `-E sgi -e ip32`, `-E alpha -e alphabook1`, `-E mvme88k -e mvme187` and `-e mvme188` **all reach `machine:` and print a cpu line**.  *A rig is what a rung-4 BOOT needs.  Conflating the two is what left both of these rows ranked as witness-blocked when neither had been tried.*  **WHAT IS STILL NOT ESTABLISHED, and the attempt is exactly for this:** whether the debugger reaches these registers through the machine's real decode, and whether the observable differs pre- and post-fix.  Constructing is necessary, not sufficient.  *My first run of this test reported all three machines failing -- because I ran the WSL-built ELF from git-bash and got `cannot execute binary file`, while my own grep for `GXemul|machine:` matched the word `gxemul` INSIDE that error and scored it a healthy banner.  A broken apparatus plus a check that matched the error text nearly filed a confident \"these machines do not construct\".*  For this row the target is **`-E alpha -e alphabook1`**, which is the only subtype whose arm calls `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`.  *** PASS-1 PACKET-FED PANEL, 2026-08-31 (`_scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346`).  THIS ROW'S OWN TITLE WAS WRONG, MY BRIEF CONTRADICTED ITSELF, AND BOTH ARE NOW SETTLED FROM THE SOURCE. ***  **(1) THE CENSUS.  The title said each register *accepts exactly ONE value*; that is true of ONE arm out of four.**  Derived, with the last constant looked up after glm named it as the settling fact:  `W_MASK0` accepts exactly `{0x700000}` -- one.  `W_BASE0` accepts `{0, 0x300800000}` -- **two**.  `IOC_CONF` kills iff `idata & ~1`, so it accepts `{0, 1}` -- **two**, and the predicate is *bit 0 only*, not a constant.  `TB_ENA` kills iff `idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN`, and `alpha_lcareg.h` defines `IOC_TB_ENA_TEN 0x0000000000000080ULL` -- a SINGLE bit, so it accepts `{0, 0x80}` -- **two**.  Title corrected.  *This matters beyond tidiness: the de-escalation rule owes an accept row per predicate, and a detector built from `exactly one value` would pin the wrong accept side on three of the four arms.*  **glm's sharpest point is about WHICH value to pin:** for `W_BASE0` the accepted `0` is the *maximally ordinary* write -- zeroing a window -- so an accept row pinned to the exotic magic constant *misfires on the most legal write rather than an unusual one*.  **(2) MY BRIEF CONTRADICTED ITSELF AND THE SOURCE SETTLES IT AGAINST ME.**  Q2 asserted these fields *feed address-window decoding*; Q3's note said the emulator *does not otherwise model them*.  glm caught that both cannot be true and asked which.  Measured (`grep -n 'window_base_0|window_mask_0|tlb_enable|ioc_conf' src/devices/dev_lca.c`): `window_base_0`, `window_mask_0` and `tlb_enable` are **written and read back and nothing else** -- each appears exactly twice, as `odata = d->X` and `d->X = idata`.  **Only `ioc_conf` is CONSUMED**, at `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1`.  *** SO THE FIX DESIGN SPLITS, AND IT WOULD NOT HAVE WITHOUT THIS CHECK. ***  Three of the four arms are pure BACKED ACCEPT storage -- a decline that keeps the store harms nothing, because no emulator code reads the field.  **The fourth is different**: `ioc_conf`'s low bit selects the bus, and its guard is `idata & ~1`, so a rejected value's bit 0 still reaches the consumer.  glm's *\"a decline that keeps the store is an accept with a complaint, so validate BEFORE storing and leave the previous value\"* is right **for that arm specifically**, and is over-general for the other three.  **(3) `idata` IS `uint64_t`** (`memory_readmax64`), so `0x300800000` is representable and glm's width worry is answered -- the comparison is reachable, not dead code.  **(4) Reachability stays a READING, correctly.**  All three seats declined to say whether these are the only values REAL HARDWARE accepts or merely the only ones this emulator was developed against, and read the code's own wording (`TODO`, *\"differs from the expected values\"*) as supporting the second.  There is no LCA datasheet in `_scratchpad/`.  *That is the honest form and it is flagged as a reading rather than a fact.*\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP MEASUREMENT 2026-08-31 (not a seat answer; recorded here rather than in `entries` so it cannot be counted toward the panel). ***  evidence: dev_lca.c :203 :222 :237 :252 :276 :291 (six guards); :129 inside a comment block\n\n*** THREE WRONG RECORDS ON THIS ROW, found by the rung-3 seat and RE-VERIFIED BY READING dev_lca.c before being written here. ***\n\n(1) SCOPE: there are SIX value-guarded arms, not four.  Verified at dev_lca.c :203 (idata & ~1, IOC_CONF), :222 (idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN, TB_ENA), :237 (W_BASE0), :252 (W_MASK0), :276 (W_BASE1), :291 (W_MASK1).  OUTSTANDING_BUGS scoped four-plus-two-masks (= the correct six); the LEDGER's pass-1 census picked a DIFFERENT four and silently dropped W_BASE1 and W_MASK1.  A detector built from the ledger's four ships NO ACCEPT ROW for those two, which the de-escalation clause requires per predicate.\n\n(2) THE EARLIER TITLE CORRECTION IS ITSELF WRONG.  It says the one-value claim is `true of ONE arm out of four'.  Against all six, THREE arms accept exactly one value: W_MASK0 {0x700000}, W_BASE1 {0x240000000}, W_MASK1 {0x3ff00000}.  W_BASE0 accepts two, IOC_CONF {0,1}, TB_ENA {0,0x80}.  **The original title was closer to right than the correction that overturned it** -- this project's own rule that a correction carries more authority than the error and therefore earns a SECOND reading.\n\n(3) hold_question says TWELVE live exit(1).  It is ELEVEN: `grep -c` returns 12, but dev_lca.c:129 is inside a /* ... */ block.  The row's own `witness' field already said eleven, so the row contradicted itself.\n\nCONSUMPTION, confirmed and stronger than filed: five of the six guarded arms are PURE STORAGE (each field appears exactly twice -- odata = d->X and d->X = idata).  `ioc_conf' is the sole consumer, at dev_lca.c:121 `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1'.",
regress/artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:6268:tools/pipeline/ledger.json-28111-   "title": "a 64-bit guarded value printed through (int) in a KILL diagnostic, so the message can name a value that would NOT have killed -- and at one site names the value the code calls ACCEPTABLE.  A CLASS, censused; derive the population, do not quote it",
regress/artifacts\m88kexcdeliver\pass5_panel_20260906_090810\codex.txt:6442:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:28747:   "note": "DEFECT, HIGH.  cpu_m88k.c, anchor `vector < M88K_EXCEPTION_USER_TRAPS_START && !implemented': only RESET/INTERRUPT/INSTRUCTION_ACCESS/DATA_ACCESS set `implemented = true'; nine other switch cases print a VERBOSITY_ERROR and break, so the fallback sets running=false on every CPU and returns (emul.c then breaks the main loop: a GRACEFUL guest-drivable machine halt, not an abort).  Pre-existing upstream (`git log -L' on the fallback returns only the 0.7.0 import); exposed by the #380 review, NOT introduced by #323/#380.  Reachable classes by raise site (deriver: grep -o 'm88k_exception(cpu, *M88K_EXCEPTION_[A-Z0-9_]+' src/cpus/cpu_m88k*.c | sort | uniq -c): PRIVILEGE_VIOLATION 10 (ldcr/stcr/xcr/rte/ld.usr/st.usr/tb0/tb1/tcnd in user mode), ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE 4 (divu/div by zero, any mode), INTEGER_OVERFLOW 3 (add/sub, any mode), MISALIGNED_ACCESS 1, UNIMPLEMENTED_OPCODE 1 (prom_call in user mode); BOUNDS_CHECK and ERROR have no LITERAL-CONSTANT raise site -- but a supervisor tb0/tb1/tcnd names any 9-bit vector directly (`m88k_exception(cpu, ic->arg[2], 1)'), so both ARE reachable today (MEASURED on pass 1: supervisor `tb1 0,r3,7' halts HEAD; an earlier wording here said 'NO raise site today (latent)' -- corrected by #502's pass 1).  ERROR is additionally reached by the SFRZ re-route when a handler faults.  Vector offsets verified against Table 6-1 (mc88100.txt:8524-8538): PRIVILEGE 0x30, ILLEGAL_DIVIDE 0x40, OVERFLOW 0x48, MISALIGNED 0x20.  DISPOSITION: DELIVER (not a device-style decline -- the guest OS has handlers for these): `implemented = true' for the five, flowing through the existing common path (SFRZ, EPSR, SXIP/SNIP/SFIP shadow, supervisor switch, pc = VBR + 8*vector); MISALIGNED already fills DMT in the load/store template.  WITNESS OWED AT RUNG 3 BEFORE ANY EDIT: cold-debugger probe on committed testm88k or luna-88k (exemplars regress/m88k_idle_probe.py, m88k_rounding_probe.py): supervisor `divu r2,r3,r0', VBR into RAM, breakpoint at VBR+0x40, free-run -- pre-fix `unimplemented vector=0x008' and the machine stops; post-fix PC == VBR+0x40, SXIP = faulting instruction | XIP_V, PSR shadow frozen, machine running; plus a LIVENESS control (an already-delivered vector) and an ATTRIBUTION control (the same instruction on a non-faulting operand does not vector).  Split from m88kexcsfu1 (one fix design per round).  Ranked at the top of the live-defect tier, just below the exit(1)-abort class (this one stops gracefully).  Provenance: retrospective re-check (owner directive 2026-09-04) batch 2 -- GPT-6 Astra (codex, max effort) read-only review, adjudicated by Fable 5.1; artifacts regress/artifacts/retro_20260905/batch2_m88k/ (astra_batch2.txt, fable_adj_batch2.md).  The other seven panel seats were DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED on the retrospective (owner scope); the fix round runs the full nine-seat panel.  ROUND #502 (2026-09-05): SEVEN classes delivered (the five plus BOUNDS and ERROR), at BOTH sites (switch-1 `implemented' AND a tail `case' -- the one-site form was MEASURED to stop the cpu at the tail default).  Detector regress/m88k_exc_probe.py v3 (22 fault rows + 2 controls; rows resid50/sfu1h EXPECT HALT as negative controls) wired into gate 11; HEAD 0/9 -> 0/10 -> 3/22 (the two HALT controls + the pre-existing user vector 128), fix 9/9 -> 10/10 -> 22/22.  Three kill tables + the Opus pass-2 table under regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/: table 1 exposed M_ERRORDROP (repaired: errortb), the Opus pass-2 table exposed M_NOVBR / M_SFRZTRAP / M_SFIPNOTGT / M_DSLOTSWAP (all 10/10; repaired: vbrtb, trapfrozen, dslot br.n +16), pass-2 seats exposed the trap-route blindness (repaired: misaligntb/unimpltb/div0tb/ovftb/privsuptb), the carry half of F14 (ovfc), the LEVEL channel (Fable: INFO vs ERROR IS observable at default verbosity), the `<=' tail-guard bound (usertb) and the missing negative controls (kimi: resi