regress/artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:437:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-06 from the `sgirandompixel' design panel, where **codex argued for it as part of that round\nand grok argued for filing it -- and grok's scoping was taken**, because a return value plus every GET site\nis not ONE SITE and the reopening rule says a round takes one.\n\nAfter `sgirandompixel', `horrible_getputpixel()' leaves `*color' alone on a declined GET, so the caller's\nown `uint32_t color = fg' stands and the PUT writes the FOREGROUND.  That is DETERMINISTIC, which is the\nwhole gain of that round -- and it is still a write of data the guest did not ask for.\n\n**codex's form, recorded verbatim because it is the design if this is taken:** give the function an explicit\nSUCCESS RESULT; *'unchanged `*color' is merely an output-parameter guarantee: callers must not consume it\nfollowing failure'*.  Cover ALL the GET sites -- the source GET, the `oldcolor' GET used by ROPs, and MTE's\n`bg' GET.  **AND ITS WARNING, which is the sketch rule and is the reason this is not a one-liner:**\n*'preserve coordinate advancement and stipple rotation when suppressing a pixel's PUT; an early `continue'\ncan bypass those updates'* -- anchors `src_x += step_x' and `Rotate the stipple pattern'.\n\nThe shipped detector already discriminates this: `lin_b is fg' expects fg TODAY, and under a skip-PUT fix it\nwould expect the PRE-PAINT instead.  So the oracle-correction rule applies again and the row set is known in\nadvance.",
regress/artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:477:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-06.  `horrible_getputpixel(int put, ..., int mode)' is called with `dst_mode' by the PUT sites\nand with `src_mode' by the XFER GET, but its diagnostic hardcodes the word `dst_mode' -- so the message a\nreader sees for a failed SOURCE read names the wrong register.\n\n**DELIBERATELY NOT FIXED IN `sgirandompixel', and the reason is worth keeping.**  That round DID reword it,\nwhich broke the probe's grep anchor and turned two rows red on the fixed build for a reason unrelated to the\nfix.  Moving the anchor to match would have been the easy repair and is **the laundering shape**: changing\nan oracle after seeing a red row cannot be told from fitting the test to the binary.  The wording went back\nand the inaccuracy is filed.\n\nWhoever takes this changes the message AND the probe's anchor in ONE diff, and says so.",
regress/artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:506:Delete `*color = random();`. Keep the diagnostic, latch it, `return` without touching `*color`. Same shape as `#500`'s `SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP` arm, and the same `*color` contract as every other failure in this function (`x/y` OOB, `tile_nr >= 256`, linear `tile_nr >= 32`, invalid `tileptr` bit 31). Next free bit is `1 << 10` (the `SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP` comment says so on purpose). One bit, not two: one site, one format string; `(once)` in the text.
regress/artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:605:  (i) member 3 minimal vs codex's fuller form now. Taking the fuller form in-round would change a void function's signature and touch every GET site (LINE oldcolor GET, RECT XFER GET, MTE bg GET — three sites, plus coordinate-advance/stipple-rotation hazards codex named). The house rule "a round takes one site" — defensible; residual filed with codex's design verbatim. Not wrong.
regress/artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:619:  Also check: does the announce text keep "dst_mode" wording — yes, and it's filed (sgirenamedstmode) with the laundering rationale. And the probe anchors on "unimplemented dst_mode" — present in the shipped message. Consistent.
regress/artifacts\sgirandompixel\pass2_panel\kimi.txt:675:  **The five candidates:** (i) member 3 minimal is grok's verdict verbatim in `design_panel/grok.txt`, and codex's fuller form (success result, all three GET sites, coordinate/stipple hazard) is preserved verbatim in ledger row `sgirandomskipput` — filing it is the house one-site rule working, not evasion. (ii) `fg` over zero is right: zero is a fabricated pixel of the same class as `random()`, and it matches the `tileptr` sibling's existing fail-and-write-fg behaviour. (iii) the fresh bit is the file's own measured rule (#498's 23/25 shared-bit collision), and the four handlers share one malloc'd struct (`:651-656`). (iv) `lin_b not src` is not vacuous in the one direction that matters: it is the only row that reddens if someone silently takes `sgirelinb` (dest would equal SRC_VAL) — a regression guard against the ledger, correctly labelled non-discriminating for this fix. (v) all five residuals verified in `tools/pipeline/ledger.json` — present, `state: open`, opened 2026-09-06, with substantive notes.

