regress\pass2_ledger.tsv:40:a29f87b	2026-08-21	panel_20260821_112116	8 fired: 7of7 scriptable answered + opus5; fable5 DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (owner directive), not a seat failure	7of7	SHIP-WITH-CHANGES, applied before this receipt. *** FIVE OF SEVEN SCRIPTABLE SEATS SAID SHIP WITH CHANGES, and FOUR produced an eighth mutant INDEPENDENTLY -- all one class: the detector is construction-only and asserted almost nothing about the CONTENT of the device string. S1 pinned ONE ARGUMENT TOKEN; every other field was free. *** GROK'S DEFEATS S1 ON ITS OWN GROUND: move the wrongness from the ARGUMENT to the FORMAT -- `vrip.%i` -> `vrip.10` -- leaving VRIP_INTR_SIU exactly where it is. S1 inspects the argument list, sees the constant passed whole, reports 'plain', and passes. The row added specifically to close the wrong-but-registered-line class did not close it. SIBLINGS, one field over each: deepseek's addr_mult=4 -> addr_mult=1 (the UART maps at wrong offsets and construction still succeeds); minimax's general statement that nothing in the 26 rows touched the value of the rest of the string; and glm's deletion of the console guard. CLOSED BY TWO ROWS. S4 pins each arm's WHOLE device string by EXACT MATCH against a table -- deliberately not a pattern, because a pattern is what S1 is and the lesson of this pass is that a pattern pins what its author thought of. A field-by-field row set would have had exactly the shape that just failed. Cost accepted deliberately: a legitimate change to any arm's string turns S4 red and must update the table in the same edit. S4 killed THREE of the four. *** GLM'S ESCAPED S4 BECAUSE IT IS NOT IN THE DEVICE STRING AT ALL *** -- deleting the `if (!machine->x11_md.in_use)` test leaves the assignment unconditional, so under X11 the ns16550 takes the console handle dev_vr41xx_init's KIU console should hold. That is precisely the behaviour #444's reorder was written to PRESERVE. Measured 27/27 before S5. It cannot be caught behaviourally here -- THIS PROBE NEVER EXERCISES X11 and a headless run cannot -- which a seat named independently before naming the mutant. S5 is a source-text row and says so. MEASURED after both: grok 27/28, deepseek 27/28, glm 27/28, minimax 27/28, clean tree 28/28. A DEFECT IN MY OWN FIRST DRAFT OF S4, caught by running it: the extractor read device_add()'s arguments, but THE LITERAL IS NOT THERE -- every arm builds the string with snprintf into tmpstr and passes the buffer. It found nothing and the row compared empty lists, going red for the wrong reason. Now reads the snprintf format and concatenates its adjacent literals. Gates: gate_hygiene PASS (48). EXPECT_ROWS 26 -> 28.
regress\pass2_ledger.tsv:41:bcdbfb8	2026-08-21	panel_20260821_164002	8 fired: 7of7 scriptable answered + opus5 measuring seat; fable5 DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (owner directive), not a seat failure	7of7	SHIP THE FIX, REPLACE THE DETECTOR -- and the detector was replaced before this receipt was filed. 8 seats fired, 7of7 scriptable answered (codex, agy, kimi 69KB, grok, glm, deepseek, minimax) plus the opus5 measuring seat; fable5 DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED per the owner's standing deprioritisation, recorded as a deliberate omission and never as agreement. SIX of the seven returned DEFECT-IN-DETECTOR; minimax said SOUND-with-the-detector-as-the-weaker-link, which is the same finding under a different label. *** THE MEASURING SEAT BUILT SIXTEEN MUTANTS AND ALL SIXTEEN SCORED 7/7. *** The champion is TWO CHARACTERS: `ETHERNET_STRING_MAXLEN` -> `0*ETHERNET_STRING_MAXLEN`. snprintf with size 0 writes NOTHING, not even the NUL, so the heap-buffer-overflow returns on ip12/ip28/ip30/ip35 -- ASan-measured back to the pre-fix numbers -- and it compiles with ZERO warnings while the gate row printed green. Also 7/7: size 1 (writes only the NUL -> EMPTY MAC, ASan silent), sizeof(eaddr_string) (=8 -> "08:20:3"), `#if 0`, a comment wrapper, `if (0)`, `if (subtype == 32)`, a second malloc, an arm re-pointing the pointer, and `#define snprintf(b,l,...) ((void)(b))`. *** AND THE REDUCTIO THAT SETTLES IT: A 217-BYTE FILE CONTAINING NOTHING BUT A C COMMENT SCORED 7/7. *** No function, no MACHINE_SETUP, nothing compilable. That is VACUOUS under this project's taxonomy, not weak -- and no additional regex repairs it, because the failure is the MEDIUM and not the rows. THE OLD FILE'S OWN STATED PURPOSE WAS THE THING IT DID NOT DO. Its docstring called itself "the LENGTH/FORMAT oracle" that sees what ASan cannot. IT NEVER EXAMINED THE LENGTH ARGUMENT. The size-1 mutant is precisely the `eaddr_string[0]='\0'` hole it named as its reason for existing, spelled with an snprintf, and it passed. Three tracked copies of that claim (probe docstring, CHANGELOG, gate comment) were corrected under the grep-for-siblings rule. REPLACED WITH A RUNTIME VALUE ORACLE, 10 rows: break on arcbios_init() in a real construction of the five reaching subtypes and require the ethernet STRING to be the formatting of the MAC BYTES passed beside it -- a provenance check, so garbage, empty, truncated and right-shaped-wrong-octet all fail. MEASURED kill table (PASSING rows out of 10; clean = 10/10): `0*` 5/10, size-1 5/10, sizeof 5/10, `#if 0` 5/10, comment 5/10, pre-fix 5/10, wrong-last-octet 6/10. Every mutant that beat the old file dies. *** V4 KILLED NOTHING IN THAT TABLE AND THAT WAS CORRECT, SO A DISCRIMINATING MUTANT WAS BUILT FOR IT. *** ip32 has its own snprintf in its arm and is rescued from every mutation of the pre-switch fill. An untested row is indistinguishable from a vacuous one, so both fills were removed: 4/10, V4 fires. No row now goes unexercised. GATE MOVED, AND THE MOVE IS THE POINT: the old detector lived in gate_offline because source text needs no binary. The replacement needs build/gxemul and gdb, and gate_offline RUNS NO EMULATOR AT ALL (zero references to the binary) -- adding one would have made the gate's own name untrue. It went to gate 9, which already constructs every machine and subtype: ASan is that gate's instrument for the memory-safety class, this probe is its instrument for the VALUE class ASan cannot see. check_probe_wiring.py followed the move with no edit, because it DERIVES wiring from run.sh's GATES array rather than asserting it. Nothing was left behind in gate_offline: a stub row asserting the probe merely EXISTS cannot fail for the reason it names. degrade() not gate_skip() on a missing gdb -- gate_skip EXITS and would have discarded the sweep's own eleven checks and reported the whole gate as SKIP. THREE WRONG RECORDS FOUND IN MY OWN MATERIAL, all corrected. (1) The `:633` citation had drifted to :661 -- the read-the-line-before-citing-it rule. (2) The added source comment said "machine.c forces machine->bootarg non-NULL"; machine.c:559 is a DEBUG PRINT whose local has_bootarg only selects which message is emitted, and bootarg is not on this path at all. The CONCLUSION (only defective caller) survives on the two-caller grep, which agy and grok both performed independently; the argument for it did not. (3) "callee-side hardening is an IMPOSSIBILITY" overreached: impossible AT THAT SIGNATURE, not categorically. *** THE UBSAN FOLLOW-UP UNDERCOUNTED ITSELF BY FIVE TIMES. *** It was filed as "two left-shift defects at arcbios.c:737/784". The measuring seat found ip32 never reaches those lines -- its two hits are :570 and :590 -- making it four sites. A grep for the idiom then found TEN unguarded `(buf[3]<<24)` sites and FOUR correctly cast `((uint32_t)buf[3]<<24)`. Line 580 sits BETWEEN 570 and 590 and is the only one of the three that is cast: the author knew the idiom and applied it to 4 of 14 sites. :737 is the damaging one -- the sign-extended low word is ADDED to high-word terms, so the 64-bit pointer's high word comes out off by one for every address with bit 31 set. A SEAT'S FACTUAL CHALLENGE WAS REFUTED BY EXECUTION WHILE ITS MECHANISM CLAIM WAS SIMULTANEOUSLY RIGHT. grok read the arms and reported ip27 has no abort(), so "five call abort()" overstates. MEASURED over all ten advertised subtypes: ip19/20/22/24/27 return 134 and ip12/28/30/32/35 return 1 -- the five ARE the five. But ip22 has no body and FALLS THROUGH into case 24, and ip27 dies under gdb at interrupt_handler_lookup() <- devinit_z8530() <- device_add(), on its own arm's irq=0 -- which is exactly what grok predicted. A token search of a switch cannot see fall-through, and reading for abort() misses an abort raised by a callee; my own case-splitter had the identical blind spot. kimi audited the kill-table ARITHMETIC rather than the code and challenged "four-octet MAC 6/7 (E5 alone)" as inconsistent. MEASURED: format-only mutant 6/7 with E5 the sole failure; format-AND-arguments mutant 5/7 with E5 and E6. Both numbers right, different mutants -- and the real defect it exposed is that "(E5 alone)" never says whether E5 alone FIRED or alone SURVIVED, and the two readings are opposite. A kill-table cell must name the direction. STILL OPEN AND DECLARED: the ip32 arm's own snprintf is now redundant; kept, because deleting it drops the buffer to a single write site and costs nothing to leave. Nothing here claims anything about the bytes the GUEST reads back -- that needs a rung-3 probe on a machine that gets that far, and there is no SGI rig. The probe reads arguments from SysV AMD64 registers because build/gxemul has no DWARF; that ties it to one ABI, stated in the file rather than hidden, with V6/V7 as the rows that would catch a wrong register rather than let it read as zero. Gates: gate_offline PASS (263), gate_asan_sweep PASS (13, was 11), each run singly.
