 3394 **VERDICT: IMPLEMENT, with revisions. Your device shift is correct. Type-1 must also decode the bus from the tag, and the armed all-ones row cannot prove implementation.**
 3395 
 3396 1. **Q1: `dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f` is correct.**
 3397 
 3398    In `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)`, the comment's `tag` is the value **before address construction shifts it left**. The existing `relative_addr >> 5` reverses that construction; `& ~0xff` then removes the register-offset bits. The low `0x3 << 3` access encoding disappears during the right shift. Consequently, device bits are now **15..11**, exactly as your sketch assumes. Existing `func` and `reg` extraction remain correct for those fields. [dev_lca.c:98](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:98)
 3399 
 3400    Independent corroboration: the historical OpenBSD driver's `lca_conf_read()` constructs the access with `tag << 5`; its `lca_decompose_tag()` extracts device with `>> 11`. [lca_pci.c:97](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/alpha/pci/lca_pci.c:97)
 3401 
 3402 2. **Q2: `IOC_CONF` selects the configuration format; it does not supply the target bus number.**
 3403 
 3404    Real LCA copies `IOC_CONF[1:0]` onto PCI `AD[1:0]`. Those encode type 0 (`00`) or type 1 (`01`). The target bus is a separate type-1 address field. [LCA hardware manual, 6.4.2](https://ftp.zx.net.nz/pub/archive/ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/DECinfo/semiconductor/literature/21066hrm.pdf#page=228), [PCI specification, 3.2.2.3.1](https://lekensteyn.nl/files/docs/PCI_SPEV_V3_0.pdf#page=48)
 3405 
 3406    For ordinary nonzero-bus type-1 cycles, the replacement in `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)` therefore needs **both** assignments:
 3407 
 3408    ```c
 3409    } else {
 3410        bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff;
 3411        dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f;
 3412    }
 3413    ```
 3414 
 3415    Your sketch otherwise routes every type-1 request to bus 1. Keep the **branch selection based on `ioc_conf`**; extracting the bus first and branching on that would misinterpret overlapping type-0 IDSEL bits. [dev_lca.c:121](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:121)
 3416 
 3417 3. **Q3: IMPLEMENT is justified, but specify its limits.**
 3418 
 3419    `bus_pci_setaddr()` stores the supplied tuple, and `bus_pci_data_access()` matches the complete bus/device/function tuple against its device list. That supports implementing the decode without redesigning the backend. [bus_pci.c:192](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/bus_pci.c:192), [bus_pci.c:99](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/bus_pci.c:99)
 3420 
 3421    **Premise 4's absence contract is overstated.** In `bus_pci_data_access()`, an absent target produces:
 3422 
 3423    | Operation | Existing behavior |
 3424    |---|---|
 3425    | Read at register 0 | `0xffffffffffffffff` |
 3426    | Read at another register | Zero |
 3427    | Write | Calls `fatal()` on every access, then returns |
 3428 
 3429    There is no diagnostic latch in that branch. `fatal()` itself returns. [bus_pci.c:109](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/bus_pci.c:109), [debugmsg.c:384](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/core/debugmsg.c:384)
 3430 
 3431    Thus explicitly returning all-ones everywhere already differs from delegation on an empty bus. A deliberate absence response is a defensible containment choice, but its observable contract needs stating.
 3432 
 3433    **Also define type-1 requests encoding bus 0.** The two assignments above decode fields; they do not enforce routing. Forwarding such requests directly can reach primary-bus entries because the backend receives no cycle-type information. LCA already populates a primary-bus device in `DEVINIT(lca)`. I recommend treating type-1/bus-0 requests as absent in this model. PCI type-1 transactions are forwarded through bridges to another bus segment; ordinary local targets ignore them. [dev_lca.c:389](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:389), [PCI specification, p. 49](https://lekensteyn.nl/files/docs/PCI_SPEV_V3_0.pdf#page=49)
 3434 
 3435 4. **Q4: Add the armed signature, but it remains an absence-result test.**
 3436 
 3437    It distinguishes all-ones from a successful return that leaves the read buffer untouched: `MEMORY_RW_ONE_PAGE()` clears that buffer before device dispatch. [memory_rw.c:485](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/cpus/memory_rw.c:485)
 3438 
 3439    It still conflates correct delegation, explicit all-ones, and incorrect decoding that happens to select another absent target. Diagnostic policy also needs a separate oracle.
 3440 
 3441    Two details in the existing witness matter:
 3442 
 3443    - Its top-level setup accesses **configuration offset zero**. In type-1 format that encodes **bus 0**, not bus 1. The supplied measurement proves the mode-selected termination defect, but does not validate secondary-bus routing. [lca_confbus_probe.py:167](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/lca_confbus_probe.py:167)
 3444    - `printed()` uses `re.search()`, selecting the **first** register print. Merely appending another `print t2` and calling the same parser would recheck the control result. Parse the armed result independently. [lca_confbus_probe.py:157](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/lca_confbus_probe.py:157)
 3445 
 3446    **The missing discriminating measurement:** an isolated handler fixture with distinguishable configuration contents at the same device/function on buses 1 and 2. Verify that a bus-2 request returns bus-2 contents, with nonzero device, function, and register fields; verify write dispatch separately. That tests the `lca_pci_conf`  `bus_pci_setaddr`  `bus_pci_data_access` path against both canned absence and your sketch's fixed-bus mistake.
 3447 
 3448 5. **Q5: This closes only the selected configuration-path termination.**
 3449 
 3450    The following remain independent:
 3451 
 3452    - `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc)` default exits for reads and writes. [dev_lca.c:351](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:351)
 3453    - `lca_interrupt_assert()` and `lca_interrupt_deassert()` still exit. [dev_lca.c:70](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:70)
 3454    - `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc)` still exits for rejected `IOC_CONF` bits; `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_isa)` has another explicit exit on its offset/length guard. [dev_lca.c:229](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:229), [dev_lca.c:193](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:193)
 3455 
 3456 6. **Q6: The hardware all-ones premise also needs correction.**
 3457 
 3458    Real LCA's master-abort behavior specifies arbitrary read data with error status, an instruction exception, and error reporting/logging-not a guaranteed successful all-ones load. [LCA hardware manual, 6.2.18](https://ftp.zx.net.nz/pub/archive/ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/DECinfo/semiconductor/literature/21066hrm.pdf#page=211)
 3459 
 3460    The historical OpenBSD `lca_conf_read()` initializes its result to all-ones and uses `badaddr()` before reading. That distinction matters: the emulator's measured ID signature is a compatibility contract, not evidence that full LCA absence/error semantics are implemented. [lca_pci.c:142](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/alpha/pci/lca_pci.c:142)
 3461 
