 8723 
 8724 6.2.1 CPU Request Queue
 8725 
 8726             The CPU request queue is used for all CPU-initiated PCI transfers and for
 8727             CPU accesses to registers that are internal to the IOC. The two-entry CPU
 8728             request queue can hold an address and up to one quadword of write data for
 8729             CPU-initiated PCI transactions as follows:
 8730 
 8731             ? Up to two CPU-initiated PCI write transfers
 8732 
 8733             ? Only one CPU-initiated PCI read transfer or one CPU access to an IOC
 8734                   register (read or write)
 8735 
 8736             ? One CPU-initiated PCI write transfer in the first entry and one CPU-
 8737                   initiated PCI read transfer or one CPU access to an IOC register in the
 8738                   second entry
 8739 
 8740 6.2.2 Requesting PCI Mastership
 8741 
 8742             When a CPU-initiated PCI read or write cycle is queued in the IOC and the
 8743             IOC grant signal gnt_l is not asserted, the IOC asserts its bus request signal
 8744             req_l. External arbitration logic is required to arbitrate requests from the
 8745             various PCI devices in the system.
 8746 
 8747             When the IOC gnt_l signal is asserted and the PCI bus is idle, the IOC
 8748             assumes bus ownership. (The PCI bus is idle when both the frame_l and
 8749             irdy_l signals are sampled deasserted.) In the next cycle, the IOC initiates a
 8750             PCI transfer (Section 6.2.5).
 8751 
 8752                                                        I/O Controller 6?5
 8753             The IOC automatically deasserts its req_l signal in the same cycle that it
 8754             initiates the PCI bus cycle and asserts its frame_l signal. If another request
 8755             is queued in the IOC and the IOC gnt_l signal is not asserted (that is, the
 8756             PCI bus is not parked with the CPU), the IOC will not reassert its req_l
 8757             signal until one cycle after the completion of the current IOC-initiated PCI
 8758             transaction.
 8759 
 8760 6.2.3 Default Mastership (Bus Parking)
 8761 
 8762             The PCI Local Bus Specification, Revision 2.0 defines bus parking as granting
 8763             the PCI bus to a device when no requests are pending.
 8764 
 8765             Digital recommends that the arbiter grant bus ownership to the CPU by
 8766             asserting the IOC gnt_l signal when no devices are requesting bus mastership.
 8767             This reduces the latency for CPU-initiated PCI transfers when the bus is idle.
 8768 
 8769             If the IOC is granted the bus when it is not asserting its req_l signal, it
 8770             enables the ad<31:0>, c_be_l<3:0>, and par output drivers one cycle after its
 8771             gnt_l signal is asserted to prevent the drivers from floating. If the IOC has
 8772             not initiated a PCI transfer, it tristates these output drivers one cycle after its
 8773             gnt_l signal is deasserted.
 8774 
 8775             If the IOC gnt_l signal is asserted, the PCI is idle (both the frame_l and
 8776             irdy_l signals are sampled deasserted), and a request is queued in the IOC,
 8777             the IOC initiates a PCI transaction on the next PCI cycle without asserting its
 8778             req_l signal.
 8779 
 8780 6.2.4 Memory Barrier Instruction Requirements
 8781 
 8782             The memory barrier (MB) instruction must be used to guarantee strict write-
 8783             ordering and prevent write-merging operations in the internal CPU write
 8784             buffer when not addressing dense space. (Merging in the write buffer is
 8785             permitted for dense memory space.) When executed, the MB instruction forces
 8786             any data in the internal CPU write buffers to be flushed.
 8787 
 8788             Because a shifted address is used to access PCI sparse memory spaces,
 8789             the behavior of the internal write buffer is important. Write operations to
 8790             addresses on the PCI that differ by at least a byte address will be written to
 8791             different hexword entries in the internal CPU write buffer. Write operations
 8792             to the same PCI address with different length encodings will be written to
 8793             different quadwords within the same hexword write buffer entry. However, the
 8794             IOC accepts only a longword or a quadword of data from any write buffer entry.
 8795             Therefore, to ensure correct operation, write operations to the same PCI sparse
 8796             address using different length encodings (byte, word, tribyte, and unmasked)
 8797             require the use of memory barrier instructions between each write operation.
 8798             In addition, write operations with the same length encoding to the same PCI
 8799 
 8800 6?6 I/O Controller
 8801             byte address will get merged in the internal CPU write buffer if they are not
 8802             separated by memory barrier instructions.
 8803 
 8804 6.2.5 Address Phase
 8805 
 8806             All PCI transfers begin with the address phase. If the cycle immediately after
 8807             the IOC detects both a bus idle condition and the assertion of the gnt_l signal,
 8808             the IOC initiates a PCI transaction as follows:
 8809 
 8810             ? Drives the PCI target address on the ad<31:0> pins
 8811 
 8812             ? Drives a valid cycle code on the c_be_l<3:0> pins
 8813 
 8814             ? Asserts the frame_l signal
 8815 
 8816             ? Drives even parity for the address on the par pin in the next cycle
 8817 
 8818             If any PCI device detects an address parity error, it reports it on the PCI serr_l
 8819             signal two cycles after the frame_l signal is asserted. (See Section 6.2.12 for
 8820             more information.)
 8821 
 8822                                                                  Note
 8823 
 8824                   Because the chip does not provide an serr_l signal pin, external logic
 8825                   must report serr_l assertions on one of the chip's irq<2:0> interrupt
 8826                   signal pins.
 8827 
 8828             Configuration cycles use address stepping, which drives the address and
 8829             command for two consecutive cycles. The frame_l signal is asserted during the
 8830             second cycle.
 8831 
 8832 6.2.6 Sparse Memory Space
 8833 
 8834             The sparse memory space is the region in which maskable, CPU-initiated PCI
 8835             transactions occur. This region uses the low bits of the CPU address to indicate
 8836             transfer size and uses the IOC_HAE register to extend the shifted address to
 8837             cover the 32-bit PCI address space.
 8838 
 8839 6.2.6.1 Generating PCI Addresses for Sparse Space
 8840             CPU physical address bits <31:7> are used for PCI address bits <26:2>. To
 8841             support various transfer size (byte enable) combinations in sparse space, the
 8842             low-order bits of the CPU physical addresses that reference PCI address spaces
 8843             are encoded as follows:
 8844 
 8845             ? Physical address bits <6:3> generate the PCI byte enable c_be_l<3:0>
 8846                   signals. Byte enables generated by combinations of physical address bits
 8847                   <6:3>, not shown in Table 6?3, are unspecified.
 8848 
 8849                                                                                                                 I/O Controller 6?7
 8850     ? Physical address bits <4:3> also specify transfer size.
 8851 
 8852     ? Physical address bits <2:0> must be zero for transfers in sparse space.
 8853 
 8854     Table 6?3 shows how the low-order physical address bits are used to indicate
 8855     transfer size.
 8856 
