 8535             handling, registers, and interface signals.
 8536             This chapter does not duplicate information that can be found in the PCI Local
 8537             Bus Specification, Revision 2.0.
 8538 
 8539 6.1 Overview
 8540 
 8541             The IOC is the interface between peripheral devices, the CPU, and system
 8542             memory. All peripheral devices in a system based on the 21066 connect to
 8543             the CPU and system memory through the IOC. The IOC interface protocol
 8544             complies with the PCI Local Bus Specification, Revision 2.0. Peripheral chips
 8545             that also comply with that specification can be connected to the IOC without
 8546             any glue logic. However, external logic is required for interrupt arbitration,
 8547             interrupt vector generation, and DMA request arbitration.
 8548             The 21066 is not a PCI peripheral. The IOC implements the functions of a
 8549             bridge between the PCI, CPU, and system memory. These functions are not
 8550             sufficient to interface the chip as a PCI peripheral component.
 8551 
 8552 6.1.1 Scatter-Gather Mapping Support
 8553 
 8554             The IOC incorporates scatter-gather mapping logic to translate 32-bit
 8555             addresses generated by PCI bus masters to the 34-bit CPU physical address
 8556             space. The IOC implements an 8-entry translation lookaside buffer (TLB) for
 8557             fast translations. The scatter-gather map tables are stored in system memory
 8558             and are automatically read by the IOC when a translation does not hit in the
 8559             TLB.
 8560 
 8561                                                                                                                 I/O Controller 6?1
 8562 6.1.2 Sparse and Dense Memory Support
 8563 
 8564             The IOC supports both sparse and dense memory space.
 8565 
 8566             Sparse memory space is a region in which the lower bits of the CPU address
 8567             indicate transfer size, and the address is shifted down by 5 bits during the
 8568             translation from a CPU address to a PCI address. The host address extension
 8569             register (IOC_HAE, Section 6.4.1) extends the shifted address to 32 bits.
 8570             The size of transfers in this space can be byte, word, tribyte, longword, and
 8571             quadword. The CPU references sparse address space when CPU address bits
 8572             <33:32> = 10. See Section 6.2.6.1 for more information about how the low-order
 8573             CPU address bits are encoded.
 8574 
 8575             Dense memory space is a region in which only longword and quadword
 8576             transfers are supported. The CPU address is transparently mapped to the PCI
 8577             address. This uses the CPU write buffers more efficiently but restricts transfer
 8578             size capability. The CPU references dense address space when CPU address
 8579             bits <33:32> = 11. The IOC_HAE register is not required for references to this
 8580             space because the address is not shifted.
 8581 
 8582 6.1.3 PCI Parity Support
 8583 
 8584             The IOC supports PCI bus parity generation and checking. The agent driving
 8585             the PCI ad<31:0> signals on any given bus cycle is responsible for driving
 8586             even parity on the PCI par signal. During all phases when address or data
 8587             is transferred on the PCI, parity covers the ad<31:0> and the c_be_l<3:0>
 8588             signals. Bytes that are masked during a transfer must be driven to stable
 8589             levels and are included in the parity computation. During configuration cycles,
 8590             special cycles, or interrupt acknowledge cycles, all address lines must be driven
 8591             to stable levels and are included in the parity computation. The calculated
 8592             parity is driven on the PCI par signal in the cycle following the cycle that
 8593             transferred the address or data. PCI address and data parity errors are
 8594             reported through different pins.
 8595 
 8596                                                                  Note
 8597 
 8598                   Because the chip does not provide an serr_l signal pin, external logic
 8599                   must report serr_l assertions on one of the chip's irq<2:0> interrupt
 8600                   signal pins.
 8601 
 8602             Usually, all address parity errors and data parity errors during PCI special
 8603             cycles are reported with the PCI serr_l signal. Such errors detected by the
 8604             IOC (which PCI devices would normally report on serr_l) are reported to
 8605             the CPU by an internal interrupt. External logic can report the assertion of
 8606             the serr_l signal by PCI peripherals using the chip's interrupt signal pins
 8607 
 8608 6?2 I/O Controller
 8609             irq<2:0>. The serr_l signal is an asynchronous PCI signal that is usually
 8610             asserted one cycle after valid parity is driven on the par signal (that is, two
 8611             cycles after valid address or data). PCI peripherals can also use the serr_l
 8612             signal to report other severe error conditions.
 8613 
 8614             Data parity errors on all PCI transactions (except during special cycles) are
 8615             reported with the PCI perr_l signal. Only data parity errors are reported on
 8616             the perr_l line. The chip provides a signal pin to connect the perr_l signal
 8617             directly to the IOC. The perr_l signal is synchronous with the PCI clock. Data
 8618             parity errors are reported on the perr_l signal one cycle after valid parity is
 8619             driven on the par signal (that is, two cycles after valid data).
 8620 
 8621             The IOC parity disable register (IOC_PAR_DIS, Section 6.4.8) allows parity
 8622             checking and reporting to be disabled. When this register is written, the IOC
 8623             does not log or report parity errors.
 8624 
 8625 6.1.4 PCI Exclusive Access Support
 8626 
 8627             The IOC supports the PCI exclusive access protocol for peripheral-initiated
 8628             transfers with system memory (also called direct memory access or DMA). The
 8629             chip cannot generate exclusive access cycles for CPU-initiated PCI transfers.
 8630 
 8631 6.1.5 Clocking
 8632 
 8633             With the exception of the PCI reset signal rst_l, all PCI signals are
 8634             synchronous with the PCI clock signal and are sampled on the rising edge
 8635             of the PCI clock. Timing parameters for PCI signals are also specified relative
 8636             to the rising edge of the PCI clock.
 8637 
 8638             The IOC provides the pci_clk_in pin for the PCI clock input. It controls
 8639             PCI signal latching in the IOC and generates the control strobes for the IOC
 8640             outputs. The pci_clk_in signal is asynchronous to the internal CPU clock.
 8641             The PCI and CPU clock domains are synchronized in the IOC.
 8642 
 8643 6.2 CPU-Initiated PCI Cycles
 8644 
 8645             The IOC operates as a PCI master when the CPU executes a load or store
 8646             instruction that addresses a PCI peripheral. The IOC provides address
 8647             windows to the CPU that allow access to the PCI memory, I/O, and
 8648             configuration address spaces. The interrupt vector and special cycle register
 8649             (IOC_IACK_SC, Section 6.4.13) generates either a PCI interrupt acknowledge
 8650             cycle when read or a PCI special cycle when written.
 8651 
