    1 # LCA interrupt delivery: two unimplemented handlers that terminate the process
    2 
    3 **VERDICT LINE FIRST.**
    4 
    5 **The tree under review is `GXEMUL-SEC/src/`.** Ignore any other `src/` you find - there is a
    6 pristine 2021 upstream copy at the project root that will make you confidently wrong, and it has
    7 bitten this project more than once. Every line number below is from `GXEMUL-SEC/src/`.
    8 
    9 **Please do not simply ratify.** A recent panel had seven of nine seats confirm a premise a brief
   10 had stated wrongly, and on this very row's sibling you refuted two of my premises last night.
   11 **Check the premises, not just the derivations.**
   12 
   13 ## 1. THE SITE
   14 
   15 `src/devices/dev_lca.c:70-86`, complete and unedited:
   16 
   17 ```c
   18 /*
   19  *  lca_interrupt_assert():
   20  *
   21  *  Line 0 = ISA interrupt.
   22  */
   23 void lca_interrupt_assert(struct interrupt *interrupt)
   24 {
   25 	fatal("lca_interrupt_assert: TODO\n");
   26 	exit(1);
   27 }
   28 
   29 
   30 /*
   31  *  lca_interrupt_deassert():
   32  *
   33  *  Line 0 = ISA interrupt.
   34  */
   35 void lca_interrupt_deassert(struct interrupt *interrupt)
   36 {
   37 	fatal("lca_interrupt_deassert: TODO\n");
   38 	exit(1);
   39 }
   40 ```
   41 
   42 Both are registered as the machine's interrupt template at `dev_lca.c:414-415`:
   43 
   44 ```c
   45 	interrupt_template.interrupt_assert = lca_interrupt_assert;
   46 	interrupt_template.interrupt_deassert = lca_interrupt_deassert;
   47 ```
   48 
   49 `machine_alpha.c:61` does `device_add(machine, "lca")`.
   50 
   51 ## 2. SETTLED PREMISES - inlined so you need not re-derive them, but say if any is wrong
   52 
   53 * **There is NO GUARD.** Unlike every other site in this campaign, no predicate rejects a value
   54   here. The whole function body is the diagnostic and the process exit. This is why the
   55   disposition menu that fits refusal decisions - decline, latched decline, accept, backed accept -
   56   **does not apply**: there is no value being refused.
   57 * **A process exit here is not a decline. Returning without effect is also not "modelling an
   58   absent device"**: the handler was installed, so something asked for delivery.
   59 * **The architectural fault disposition is NOT available on Alpha.** `memory_rw.c`'s device-failure
   60   path carries `#ifdef MEM_MIPS` and `#ifdef MEM_M88K` arms and nothing else, and
   61   `ALPHA_KENTRY_MM` is defined with **zero consumers**.
   62 * **A related declared-but-unused constant exists in the same header**: `ALPHA_PTE_ASM`
   63   (`alpha_cpu.h:223`) has exactly one occurrence in `src/` - its own `#define`. I mention it only
   64   because it shows this header declares more than the tree implements; do not assume the two facts
   65   are connected.
   66 * **`fatal()` does NOT exit.** `debugmsg.c:384` - it has no early-out and returns normally. Only
   67   the explicit `exit(1)` terminates.
   68 * **PRIMARY SOURCE IS NOW LOCAL.** `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt` (Alpha 21066/21066A Hardware
   69   Reference Manual, EC-QC4GB-TE, 13,876 lines) was fetched yesterday and its citations were
   70   verified by reading. Relevant sections by line: **4.1.13 Interrupt Request and Enable Registers
   71   at `:175`** (HIRR `:177`, SIRR `:179`, ASTRR `:181`), **`:1551`** on the hardware interrupt
   72   request register, and **6.4.13 Interrupt Vector and Special Cycle Register at `:510`**.
   73   *I have NOT read these sections beyond their headings.* You have the file - read them.
   74 
   75 ## 3. QUESTIONS
   76 
   77 **Q1. REACHABILITY, and this is the one that decides severity.** Trace who can call the installed
   78 template. Can an ordinary guest running on `-E alpha -e alphabook1` cause an assert on line 0 (the
   79 ISA interrupt) during normal operation? Name the call path or state plainly that you could not find
   80 one. **If it is unreachable today, say so - that is a real answer and it changes the disposition.**
   81 
   82 **Q2. WHAT SHOULD THESE FUNCTIONS DO?** Read 4.1.13 and 6.4.13 in the local manual and say what
   83 the hardware's interrupt request path actually is. Then say what the minimum honest implementation
   84 is in this model, given that the fault disposition is unavailable. Distinguish clearly between
   85 what the manual states and what you are inferring.
   86 
   87 **Q3. Is there an in-tree idiom to copy?** Other chipsets in `src/devices/` implement
   88 `*_interrupt_assert`/`_deassert`. Name the closest analogue and say whether its shape transfers to
   89 LCA or whether something about LCA makes it unsuitable. **Grep for the idiom rather than reasoning
   90 from first principles - the tree's own precedents are part of the design space.**
   91 
   92 **Q4. What is the smallest edit that would make this look implemented while still being wrong?**
   93 This is the adversarial question; name a specific edit and say what observable would distinguish it
   94 from a correct one.
   95 
   96 **Q5. If a reproduction is possible, what is its shape?** A guest instruction sequence on an
   97 unmodified in-tree machine description that reaches the site. If you believe none exists, say so
   98 and give the reason - an honest "not reachable from a guest" is the answer I want if it is true.
